	 ('177982598','76561198300785269','3009850','20251230',27,15,0,0,0,'There is something deep and psychological in nature about M Dickie''s games. A work of art from the darkest recesses of the human mind that presents the player with an opportunity for introspection. A chance to look inside one''s self for answers. 

Yet, the reflection staring back may not be the answer the player is willing to accept, let alone embrace. 

In Hard Time III, I spent my 23 day sentence as Ken Ryu. Ryu''s emotionless stoicism and middle-aged appearance fittingly depicted that of an imprisoned Yakuza mob boss. Yet, the crime Ryu was convicted for was only cow tipping.

Once inside the prison, the local inmates wasted no time in threatening me. Guardsmen attempted to assert their dominance at every opportunity. But, little did they know, the convicted mafia boss was about to snap. 

Just as the prison lights lowered at lockdown, I busted out of my cell. Pummeling the cell bars with lariats until they subsided. I then used the dismantled bars as blunt instruments.

Inmates who dared enter my cell were promptly murdered.
I lit their corpses on fire in my bed.
Jim Cornette and Kenny Omega had their own prison gangs.
It''s a shame they had to wage war with me. I was a Cornette Guy. 
Vince McMahon was introduced as my cell mate.
I killed him with an N64. 
I made money by purposely breaking, then repairing toilets.
One day, I was gifted with a chainsaw.
Which I used to dismember my fellow inmates, then ate their severed limbs to restore health.
The things a guy gotta do to survive, right?
Over time, I visited other cell blocks while guardsmen tried to apprehend me.
In the South Block, I heard the most beautiful music played by a giant inmate.
Her clarinet skills sounded like the angels themselves singing.
In a show of gratitude, I killed her.
The clarinet became my new favourite weapon of choice.
The once-feared Yakuza crime lord was now rebranded as ''The Clarinet Killer''.
I would play my Clarinet before each kill and covered the sounds of screaming
with the lovely tones of music.

Eventually, I spent the last day of my sentence decorating my entire cell block. 
All of the corpses and weapons were decorated neatly in my cell. 

I was then wanted for serial murder, yet was let go by the Warden since I did my time
on good behaviour.'),
	 ('40491605','76561198137630535','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,9,'I had a great 40+ hours playing poker against robots. The conversation and interactions were funny and it was a definite improvement over the original. Although I do have three issues:

1. My cards were at times not recognized, usually flushes, straights and the odd full house, which is really infuriating when in a showdown with all my earnings on the line.

2. Many Conversations were repeated, I was ready to die when I heard about Sam living in black and white for the 7th time.
I guess the conversations are all RNG and I''m quite unlucky, but adding more conversations or decreasing odds of repeated lines would help.

3. After reaching 1 Million twice from scratch and unlocking all unlocks, collecting tokens gets dull when all you can buy are drinks for other players.

Still really good game though.'),
