	 ('13920555','76561197969880352','234710','20251230',1,0,0,0,10,'I liked Poker Night 2''s bounty system, and the banter is always good, but this actually felt like a step backward from the first game. Not being able to control how talkable the characters are is a feature that was sorely missing.

And the characters themselves seemed a bit less interesting. In general I felt like they had fewer lines of dialogue and repeated themselves more. There was also a distinctive feeling of the AI cheating on the final card flip. There were some incredibly unlikely outcomes that kept happening hand after hand after hand.

So at the end of the day if you like playing poker against an AI you''ll like this, but I actually think the first one is the better of the two.'),
	 ('33974175','76561198027999486','234710','20251230',11,0,0,0,3,'While Poker Night 2 has some additional bells and whistles over its predecessor, more important features have been removed completely.  The option to disable table talk has been removed, causing the camera to focus on specific AI players and preventing you from looking for tells in other opponents.  The difficulty setting has likewise been removed -- the game is locked into a harder difficulty than Poker Night 1, with the RNG visibly biased towards the AI players.  The gameplay options menu doesn''t actually have any options, it''s just a tutorial prompt now.

Stick to the original.'),
