	 ('47864895','76561197993531522','500670','20251230',7,0,0,0,3,'A disappointment for me, even at the on sale £1.95 price tag. For a start, for a twin-stick shooter to have no controller support in 2018 is a crime in my book as is no mouse control/support in the menu screens. That goes for practically any PC game btw. The opening cut-scene played everytime on launch is also a tad annoying and should have been sorted.

The aiming is poor and awkward with the mouse, and the satisfaction gained from killing the nondescript and characterless enemies is limited by the bland and uninspiring graphics. Explosions are a splurgy mess with no oomph and sound-effects are dominated almost entirely by the gunfire coupled with the expletives yelled from the protagonist.  

Gameplay consists entirely of keeping your index-finger pressed on the left-mouse button while running away from the boring enemies (usually) towards the edge of the arena or screen. You''ll click the right mouse button occassionally for a shotgun blast and a reload. There is a sound-effect to signal when this should happen for the player but it''s buried so deep down into the mix that you can hardly hear it and you''re mainly left relying on this by using random instincts.    

Playing out a little like Ubermosh (which I disliked as well) the only thing I liked about the game was the instant restarts and the solid leaderboard that actually works. Even the inclusion of a red "X" in the title is lame. It''s a thumbs down for me with recommendations instead going to the likes of Crimsonland, Iron Crypticle/Fisticle, Kick-Ass Commandos, Nation Red, Tesla Vs Lovecraft and Circuit Breakers (at a push).


