	 ('4052384','76561198031351571','234710','20251230',8,0,0,0,10,'This game should have been amazing. Poker Night at the Inventory was fun, and Poker Night 2 theoretically improves on every aspect of that game; the decks, tables, and themes you can purchase in-game are of an even better class than ever before, and Telltale pits you against a whole slew of characters who not only live up to, but surpass those of the first installment. On paper, everything is jolly and lovely. But when you first start up the game, you realize something is wrong...horribly wrong. The effort this game forces you to put in to earn its touted TF2 items is a far cry from the laid back nature of Poker Night 1''s system. By the time you''ve earned the fifth, and arguably best, item the game has to offer, you''ve trudged through 15 disgusting challenges the game concocts for the sole purpose of extending the longevity of the game, all at the expense of the player''s mental health. Any joy you may have derived from listening to the delightfully witty banter of characters such as Claptrap or Brock Samson will quickly turn in to teeth-gnashing fury as you feebly try to find a way to skip past all the fluff just to fail the tournament at a critical moment, with the bounties right out of your reach. What''s worse, the items you can earn are intrinsically linked to the Steam achievements for the game, which means that, for the unlucky few out there (yours truly included), playing in offline mode prevents you from reaping your hard earned rewards. The proposed solution to this issue? 
"Like we give a fuck, we already got your money, fool. Delete your save and try again" 
- Telltale Games and Valve Support. 
"P.S. You''re our bitch now, LOL"

In short, this game can only have concievably been concocted in the deepest wretches of Hell, created through an unholy union between Satan, Cthulhu, and Duma Key, and fashioned from the filthy excrement of a million aborted fetuses. Do yourself a favor and pretend the Poker Night series never made it past the first installment. Even the greatest of masochists would pale at the thought of playing this   inhumane creation.

Also, Sam is a bitch.'),
	 ('20651193','76561198137702685','375460','20251230',2,0,0,0,4,'There is clearly something wrong with this game and it''s saves. They simply are not working properly and so on'),
	 ('174685684','76561198224228984','3009850','20251230',9,0,0,0,10,'I still recommend this game but sadly it''s not as big of a mix between the 2D and old 3D version as I''d hoped; it feels like the 2D one translated to 3D.

At the end of the day the old 2D one was still really fun, so this is still better than that one just by virtue of it being 3D, although I don''t think it has a few of the core features of the old game that made it as standout as it was.

Namely;

- Unique areas. All areas feel sort of samey. The removal of intelligence makes the library and old job systems unworkable. One thing that I really miss from the old game was the toilet not having any guards whatsoever, so you could do basically anything there without being caught. Items are also not banned based on area anymore, so you can''t carry a knife in the kitchen etc, although this is balanced by guards asking a lot less about it.
- The job systems themselves have been removed in favour of interactable props, like the broom & canvas. I much prefer original Hard Time''s system of your intelligence allowing you to work a certain job; for example the highest intelligence would allow you to work as a pharmacist, lowest as a cook, etc. I thought it would be expanded upon in this rather than being thrown out, personally.
- Gangs seem to be carried over from Old School. I''ve not had enough experience in them yet, but they seem to have a lot less impact than gangs in both the original Hard Time and the 2D version. I think this could just be rectified by bringing back 5 major gangs as opposed to a large amount of small gangs. Gangs also no longer have skill prerequisites, for example in the original Hard Time, there was a gang that required 70% strength and 70% agility to join. There was one that was nerdy that you could only join if you had BELOW 70%! These unique gang decisions are gone now, which to me just sucks a bit.
- For the love of god, PAUSE DIALOGUE DURING THE ALARM! It''s actually aggravating trying to fight someone and having to mash Z midway through because they randomly started to talk about the fact that you have indeed been hitting them with a nightstick; by the end of the 3rd speech in a row you''re in a headlock and a second later you''ve got the death penalty.

I understand some stuff not being back, which is why I never mentioned it above. One thing was just some inmates naturally being pretty racist. I doubt that''d fly nowadays, which is why I imagine it was removed and I don''t really care about that.

These are the big ones for me; I''ll probably expand on this list as I play more. I don''t think it''s bad that this is more of a translation of Hard Time 2D to 3D, moreso that it really lacks that ''combining elements of both games'' that the game''s description claims, and to be wary of that if you do decide to buy the game.

The new stuff that is added is more in terms of the open world & combat; there''s absolutely tons of new guns, ranging from water guns to bazookas. The open world is massive too; I think it''s the same as Old School''s except with a few added buildings, although I''ve only briefly escaped prison to explore the city! It''s not a bad or lazy remake in any sense, it''s just not what I expected from how it''s described in the about. The OG seems written off a bit.

I hope you consider adding some of these things Mat... if you''re reading, anyways! I''ve left comments open so anyone can leave their thoughts.'),
	 ('199343433','76561198071126661','375460','20251230',1,0,0,0,10,'This game is like ordering a pizza and getting a slap in the face instead. Unfun? Check. Unbalanced? Absolutely. The official best strategy guide literally tells you to ignore the trading—you know, the ENTIRE POINT of the game—and instead gamble your life''s savings on diamond mines like you''re in some cursed Vegas slot machine simulator.

So I did it. I went full prospector. Mortgaged my imaginary house. Sailed off with high hopes and a twinkle in my eye... and came back with empty pockets, crushed dreams, and a local bandit politely stealing my last ounce of dignity.

Honestly, I’d get more enjoyment smashing my head into a drywall and hoping for drywall diamonds. At least that has better odds.'),
