	 ('34765922','76561198146964188','386990','20251230',0,0,0,0,9,'it was like reading a lite-novel. it''s pretty short but i loved it.'),
	 ('7925065','76561197996737918','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'It''s very enjoyable. It''s Texas Hold''em Poker...again. There''s also another variant called Omaha which gives more cards to a player''s hand even though only two of those are matched with the table cards. If you like poker of these sorts, then this is a fine version of them. New opponents of note and even better commentary. Eventually you''ll have heard it all and will only wish to skip it, but that''s not an option anymore.

There are unlockable sets of tables, cards, and chips that can change the entire play environment, changing knockout sequences and offering new soundtracks. Repeatedly playing poker against the same enemies is still tedious, but much more pleasant this time.

The achievements are more reasonable this time, mostly unlockables and winning a balance of $1 million ($80 thousand profit/win v. $20 thousand/loss - it doesn''t take too many wins).

There''s only one difficulty this time and it''s not exactly easy. The Omaha variant seems slightly easier than Texas Hold''em. The tell mechanic has much more depth this time too. The characters all clearly have their own sort of playing style and each feels somewhat different.

I encountered one nasty bug where if you click on the UI too fast sometimes, the game will act like it took your click and hide the UI, but the opponents will talk like you''ve not made your move yet so you end up stuck, but this was the only bug I hit.

A main reasons to get this game is for Team Fortress 2 and Borderlands 2 unlockable equipment. They are more reliable to get this time.'),
