	 ('53827373','76561198330489557','840930','20251230',1,0,0,0,10,'I just bought the Nickervision bundle and downloaded and played all of them. One thing that isn''t hard to notice is the reuse of certain ideas. Bing Bong, Ding Dong, Righty Tighty, and Pivot all use the same obstacle types, power-ups, and upgrade system, though each with a different movement mechanic. Despite different and more varied mechanics in Bit Blaster, Diamo and Orbt, I would say that Bing Bong is my favorite. 

In Bing Bong, you control a circle on the edge of the screen that continually goes up and down. There are geometric shapes that come in from the side of the screen that, if you touch them, kill you. To avoid death, you can hold space to slow down at any time. The simple control scheme creates a great push and pull of when and where to hold down the button. You go faster and are smaller when letting go of the button but slower and bigger when pressing the button. The game''s simple design makes the act of pressing a button a significant strategic decision.

I would also say that the geometric obstacles have more weight than in other games. In Righty Tighty, for instance, the shapes of the enemies don''t really matter because you move in two dimensions and can get around them. However, in Bing Bong, the difference between a horizontally elongated rectangle and a vertically elongated rectangle is huge. A longer rectangle means it will be in your way for a longer period of time, while a taller rectangle means they will be in your way for a longer section of the screen.  Understanding this difference significantly affects how you plan your course of action.


Overall, I would say that Bing Bong is the distillation of the quintessential Nickervision mechanics. Everything works well together and creates a fun time waster. To be honest I was expecting a shallow and empty design like most abstract arcade games on mobile. I was pleasantly surprised and glad I picked up these games.'),
