	 ('845040','76561197979987727','234710','20251230',1,0,0,0,10,'Let''s compare to Poker Night 1: The dialogue started repeating much earlier, and you''re no longer allowed to skip it. On the other hand, there were a couple of conversation arcs which were apprently set up to take place over the course of several sessions of play, which was interesting. I didn''t suspect I''d like the cast of characters as much as the original, especially the guys on the right, but everybody turned out to be pretty likeable. There are still table, card and now chip style unlocks, and you don''t need to win games to get them; instead, you''re given three random ''challenges'' which usually aren''t too horrible, and then completing these challenges and winning a game unlocks cool stuff for Team Fortress 2 and Borderlands 2. I guess it is a better system than the previous. Unlocking matching club motif sets actually changes the whole Inventory''s look to that theme, such as a Borderlands one and a Portal one, and that was pretty cool. The AI now seems to cheat like a bastard way more than I remember.

Uuuuhhhh you know what, I''ll just call it about even. Even if this one doesn''t have Heavy Weapons Guy in it. Actually I was kinda hoping for the Engineer this time.'),
