	 ('195439456','76561198003336480','2427430','20251230',171,10,0,0,3,'A Sanitised Shadow of the Original – S.T.A.L.K.E.R.: Call of Pripyat Enhanced Edition Fails Its Legacy

As a long-time fan of the S.T.A.L.K.E.R. series, I was eagerly awaiting this Enhanced Edition, hoping for a revitalised experience with technical improvements—not a gutted reinterpretation of a once-immersive world. Unfortunately, what we’ve received is a politically sterilised version of the original game, stripped of much of its cultural authenticity and historical nuance.

One of the most glaring changes is the complete removal of the original Russian voice acting and the erasure of Soviet-era landmarks and references. The unique atmosphere that made the Zone feel raw, gritty, and real has been scrubbed clean in favour of a sanitised version that feels hollow and disconnected from its Ukrainian and post-Soviet roots. The language, voices, and environmental cues were part of what gave S.T.A.L.K.E.R. its cult status—this version feels like it’s been processed through a geopolitical filter rather than enhanced for the fans.

Even more baffling is the game’s Terms of Service. Section 7.10 prohibits any use of the game that could be considered “racist, harassing, xenophobic, sexist, discriminatory, abusive, defamatory or otherwise offensive or illegal.” While that might sound standard in an online game with user interactions, this version doesn’t even have multiplayer. The developers actively removed it—yet still impose vague, catch-all restrictions that read like they’re meant for a social media platform, not a single-player survival game. It''s an absurd overreach that raises serious questions about player freedom and creative expression, especially for modders and fans who helped build the game''s legacy.

But it doesn’t stop there. The game also forces you to accept invasive data collection on your system. There is no opt-out—by installing and playing, you are automatically agreeing to let the developers harvest information from your machine. For a single-player game, this is outrageous. There is absolutely no justification for this level of telemetry or system scanning, and it''s a deeply concerning precedent that undermines player privacy and ownership.

In trying to make S.T.A.L.K.E.R. more “palatable” for modern sensibilities, the developers have amputated its soul. The world no longer feels grounded in the reality it once mirrored, and the very identity that made the franchise stand out has been replaced by a generic dystopia. Now add to that the overreaching TOS and the silent data collection, and what you get is not an enhancement—but an insult to both the original vision and the player base.

Verdict: If you’re a fan of the original, you’ll likely feel alienated by this re-release. This is not a love letter to the series—it’s a politically motivated rewrite with invasive, surveillance-like policies. Enhanced visuals can’t make up for a gutted atmosphere, unnecessary censorship, and heavy-handed terms. A deeply disappointing step backwards.

https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3485994958'),
