	 ('195548958','76561197992443687','2427430','20251230',3,0,0,0,1,'STEAM DECK Review:

Here is a guide for a clean 50-70 FPS on the Steam Deck with crisp visuals and best graphics settings as the default preset sucks.

Basic:
Display mode - Full Screen
Resolution - 1280x800
Vertical Sync - off

Advanced:
Motion blur - ZERO
Vision Distance - Maximum (minor FPS impact but big visual difference)
Object detail - Maximum (no FPS impact or very minor)
Texture Detail - Maximum (no FPS impact or very minor)
Anisotropic Filtering - Maximum (no FPS impact or very minor)
Antialiasing - NO AA or FXAA (no FPS impact or very minor)

Grass Detail Density - Maximum (moderate FPS impact but big visual difference)
Sun Shadow - ON (huge FPS impact but also huge visual difference)
Grass Shadow - OFF (Saves you 4-5 FPS and also removes grass shadow pop-in as the max distance for these shadows is low to medium)

Lighting Distance - Maximum (no FPS impact or very minor)
Shadow Quality - (no FPS impact or very minor)
Grass Rendering Distance - Maximum (moderate FPS impact but big visual difference)

NPC Flashlights - ON (did not test, but I think there is no FPS impact or very minor)
Detail Bump - ON (did not test, but I think there is no FPS impact or very minor)
Steep Parallax - ON (did not test, but I think there is no FPS impact or very minor)

Sun Quality - Low (saves you a few FPS with no visual difference between Maximum)
Sun Rays - Low (did not test it properly but I think it the same as sun quality)

Screen Space Shadows - ON (did not test)
Screen Space Reflections - ON (Makes the water quite better looking, turn off to save 4-5 FPS)
Async Compute - OFF (should improve FPS by using more GPU, but turning this off I get a 1 FPS increase)

Screen Space Global Illumination - OFF (saves you 4-5 FPS while there is no difference with HBAO on low)
SSAO Mode - HBAO
SSOA Quality - Low (no difference between high)
Soft Water - ON (did not test)

Death Of Field - OFF (looks bad and blurry probably also saves you a couple of FPS)
Volumetric Light - ON (did not test)
Wet Surfaces - ON (did not test)
Volumetric Smoke - On (did not test)

AMD FSR - ON (you need this to get a non blurry visual image)
FSR Sharpening - ON (you need this to get a non blurry visual image, but takes a couple of FPS)
FSR Sharpness - Maximum (you need this to get a non blurry visual image)
FSR Frame Generation - Off (feels worse with it if you are not getting 60FPS+ constantly)
FSR Mode - Native (big performance impact, but only native + Sharpening can get you a non blurry image, quality mode huge FPS increase but it looks bad)'),
