	 ('11425213','76561197983662248','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'To start: I used to add a header to these reviews, but since it sometimes made the review too long to submit, I''ve opted to leave it on my Backloggery page. Shouldn''t happen with this game, but better safe than sorry.

Gameplay: I''m not too much of a poker buff, and in fact before Poker Night at the Inventory 1, I had never played a hand of Texas Hold''Em in my life. Fortunately, unlike Poker Night 1, I was able to come into this game with at least some experience. Overall, the AI seems a lot more subtle and nuanced than in the prior game, even if the characters can still be classified into pretty much either a safe or a dangerous player. Another change that I much appreciated is that instead of bounty items (items you can win from a special match) showing up at random, you instead have to complete bounty challenges. This makes unlocking items a whole lot easier and overall more fun. Apart from that, there really isn''t much else to this game. It''s a poker game. It''s fun to play for a little bit, but poker isn''t the main draw of this game. 8/10.

Story: Well, more like writing in general, but this really is the main draw of the game. The four characters here are Sam from Sam and Max; Ashley Williams from The Evil Dead, Brock Samson from The Venture Brothers, and Claptrap from Borderlands. Hearing the banter between these characters is always a treat; at least until you hear the same dialogue five or six times. Yes, unfortunately this game still has the issue that the first game had in that jokes can be kinda run into the ground. Nevertheless, at least for the first two or so times, it''s pretty great. If you don''t end up playing the same themed matches (where you use one of each of the source material''s unlocks) every time, you shouldn''t run into too many conversation reiterations though. Even after six or so hours, there''s a lot that I haven''t seen, which more than makes up for the two or three times I''ve heard Brock realize the irony of Sam playing a poker match. 9/10.

Graphics: I''ve always been kinda torn between whether a uniform styling of the characters or them keeping their respective stylings would make the game look better. To be honest, I''m still not 100% sure. Nevertheless, the game still retains that Telltale style that many people know and love. As far as the technical side of things, the game was able to be switched to 1080p and ran perfectly well on my GTX 760, although saying that may oversell how demanding this game is. If you''ve got an Intel HD Graphics on your processor, you shouldn''t even need a GPU for this game. Apart from that, I really didn''t see any glitches in my six or so hours. 5/5.

Sound: The voice acting is very well delivered by the same people who did the character''s source material, so that''s good. As far as the music, a lot of it is atmospheric and some is taken from the source material, but it''s all pretty enjoyable and fitting. Again, there really isn''t much to talk about since there really isn''t much in the way of original music, but what is there is good enough. 4/5.

Overall scores are: 8/9/5/4; cumulative score of 8.7/10. Worth the money now, definitely when it goes on sale.'),
