	 ('65015324','76561198136214992','386990','20251230',1,0,0,0,0,'Around 7/10 recommended. Another interesting and novel take on visual novel by ebi-hime. Some caveats first: I don''t recommend reading this if you are depressed or suicidal and may be triggered. And it may also be a little bit difficult to read if your mother tongue isn''t English.

The story is pretty grim, which is in sharp contrast to the overly vivid water-painting graphics. The protagonist, Samantha, is depressed. Therefore, naturally, following her thought will be depressive. Similarly, her dialogues are very infuriating. That is, infuriating but precisely what I would say when I''m deep inside a depression episode. I especially like how the low-self-esteem thoughts are written. They hit me pretty hard. It worries me that these high-school girls can lightly bring up a variety of grim topics ranging from depression, anxiety, suicide, [spoiler]sexuality, sex, murder, to drug usage[/spoiler]  ...

Basing characters on 18th century English poets (and posing them as 21st century high school girls) is really an interesting idea, and the execution isn''t bad either. These characters aren''t really too three-dimensional (partly due to the story length limit), but aren''t merely plot devices either. That being said, since the writing style is influenced by Romantic English literature, it''s a little bit difficult to read for me as a non-native speaker. (or perhaps it''s because I''m quite unfamiliar with English literature...)

I don''t really like the music. Other than that they don''t seem to be too well-produced, they also sometimes don''t fit the scene very well, or give me a creepy feeling when it shouldn''t. Okay, maybe it really should be a little bit creepy...'),
