	 ('201243238','76561198989158219','3411440','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'

It’s not often that a game has a profound impact on your life. In fact, I never thought a game would have the power to help me through the darkest moments I’ve ever faced, but Brainrot Kart has done just that. I’m not here to just talk about the gameplay, graphics, or mechanics—I’ve come to write about a deeper connection I never thought a racing game could give me. This is a personal story of loss, resilience, and, yes, even a bit of karting.

The Downward Spiral: Divorce and the Fish

It started as an ordinary day—my wife left me. We’d been together for 12 years. I never saw it coming. She said it was because I “wasn’t the person I used to be.” Maybe I had lost my way, but in truth, I didn’t know who I was anymore. All I could think about was how much I’d messed up. We fought, I apologized, but the damage was done. She took the house, the dog, and of course, the cat. The fish, my beloved fish, remained with me—but only for a short while.

I remember the night I sat down with my pet fish, Finn, after she left. He’d always been the silent companion in my life. I never realized how much I depended on him for comfort until he too decided to leave. He was gone the next morning. No explanation. I assume it was a cruel twist of fate, but in the pit of my loneliness, I wasn’t sure if it mattered anymore.

I spiraled into a deep depression. The days blurred together, with each one feeling worse than the last. I didn’t know how to cope, and nothing seemed to bring relief. Until, one day, I stumbled across Brainrot Kart on Steam.

A New Beginning: Racing Through the Pain

I won’t lie, at first, I wasn’t sure what to expect. Brainrot Kart seemed like another kart racer, but what I didn’t anticipate was how it would transport me into a world where nothing else mattered—where my broken heart, my empty house, and my fish-less tank could be put on hold, even if only for a while. The game became my sanctuary, my safe place when the world felt too much.

At first, it was the absurdity of the game that pulled me in. Brainrot Kart is unlike any other kart racer I’ve ever played. The graphics are wild and psychedelic, characters range from anthropomorphic mushrooms to heavily tattooed sloths, and the tracks? Absolutely insane. It’s like nothing makes sense, but that’s what I needed. A break from reality. And it worked.

As I raced through vibrant, chaotic tracks, dodging flaming rubber chickens and power-ups that felt like they were made in a fever dream, I found a strange solace. The game’s over-the-top humor, combined with its genuinely thrilling racing mechanics, gave me something to focus on—something that wasn’t about the divorce or the fish. The ridiculousness of the world kept me distracted in the best way.

But Brainrot Kart wasn’t just a distraction. It was a way for me to feel alive again.

The Therapeutic Power of Kart Racing

If you’ve ever been in a dark place, you know the struggle of finding something—anything—that can make you feel like you’re worth something. For me, Brainrot Kart did just that. Every race was a tiny victory. Every lap completed felt like an achievement. I began to see myself not as the man who had lost everything but as a racer, a competitor who could come back from failure, who could win races in a world that made absolutely no sense.

I’m not saying I’ve completely healed from the pain of losing my wife or my fish (I still don’t know where Finn went, honestly, but I’ve accepted that he’s out there somewhere, living his best life). But what I’ve realized is that the game helped me reframe my sense of self. I wasn’t a man defined by loss. I was a person capable of winning in an absurd world.

As I progressed through the tracks, I began unlocking characters and power-ups, some of which were as bizarre as they were hilarious. From a rampaging potato to a sentient taco, the absurdity provided a much-needed reminder that life, even at its worst, still has its moments of humor. Even in my sadness, Brainrot Kart made me laugh. It made me forget, just for a little while, that I was alone.

On Lactose Intolerant People: A Different Perspective

You might be wondering, what does my personal journey have to do with lactose intolerance? Well, it’s a bit of an unusual tangent, but bear with me.

See, while playing Brainrot Kart and exploring the absurd world the developers have created, I couldn’t help but draw parallels to something I’ve always wondered about: why do people insist on eating dairy if they know they’re lactose intolerant?

I’m not here to judge, but it’s a fascinating thing to me. There’s something almost rebellious about it, don’t you think? The way some lactose intolerant people push through the discomfort to enjoy a slice of pizza or a bowl of ice cream, knowing full well that their body might revolt. It’s like they’re saying, “I’m not going to let my limitations define me. I’m going to live in the moment and enjoy the things I love—even if it means a little discomfort afterward.”

In a way, that’s what Brainrot Kart has done for me. It’s made me realize that, even though life has been tough, I can still find joy in the weirdest, most unexpected places. Just like someone who indulges in dairy despite their intolerance, I’ve learned to embrace the chaos and absurdity of life—and even find moments of happiness in it.

The Personal Impact: Moving Forward

When I look back on my time with Brainrot Kart, I see more than just a fun racing game. I see the tool that helped me rebuild my self-worth, even if just a little bit. I see the game that allowed me to laugh again, to find humor in the chaos, and to slowly—bit by bit—find my way out of the darkness.

The road to recovery is long. I still have my moments of doubt. The pain of my divorce and the loss of my fish are still there, lingering in the background. But with each race I win in Brainrot Kart, I feel like I’m getting closer to finding my way back to the person I want to be. And who knows? Maybe Finn, my fish, will come back into my life one day. Or maybe I’ll meet someone who loves me for who I am, flaws and all.

But for now, Brainrot Kart is my therapy. It’s the place where I’m reminded that even when life seems like it’s falling apart, there’s always a way to race toward something better.

So to anyone reading this: If you’re feeling lost, if you’ve experienced heartbreak or loss or just need something to help you laugh through the pain, I highly recommend Brainrot Kart. It might not fix everything, but sometimes, all we need is a little absurdity and a fast kart to help us find our way again.

And to those who are lactose intolerant—keep living your truth. Enjoy that ice cream. Embrace the discomfort, and keep on racing toward the joy. After all, life’s too short to let anything hold you back.'),
	 ('40162208','76561198001993793','244070','20251230',15,0,0,0,9,'The game itself is not that bad and features some interesting concepts (a turn-based flight simulator!). There is one reason I''m downvoting it.

If your Windows username contains any non-ASCII characters (anything Cyrillic, German umlauts, French accents etc.), you can''t save. Period. The game won''t tell you this, though. You''ll spend a few hours tearing through missions, accumulating experience, saving your progress religiously, then you''ll restart the game, and surprise! No saves. Please start from the beginning.

Again, I have to stress this. It''s XXI century. We''re not dealing with DOS code pages here. OK, suppose it''s so very difficult to support Unicode in a 2013 game. It''s a generous assumption, but I''m feeling generous. Why can''t you warn the user, then? Tell them you can''t save their game, because their name is wrong?
And here''s the zinger: this bug has been known for almost five years now. The developers acknowledged it, then chose to do precisely nothing about it. I''ve decided to dust this game off today, see if the bug has been fixed. It hasn''t. So I''m writing this.

Don''t buy this game. Don''t support this kind of behavior. The game is not  even _that_ good.'),
