	 ('68510233','76561197964352654','500670','20251230',1,0,0,0,0,'I have really tried to give Tormentor x Punisher shot, but I just don''t think it''s for me. It''s not even the genre, because I can dig a good high score chaser. This one just commits two cardinal sins I cannot get past.

But first, lets talk about what the game is. It''s a twin stick shooter played in a rectangle arena with infinitely spawning enemies. Everything, including you and bosses, dies in one hit. You are trying to survive while also scoring the most points possible. If you get hit, no retries. No lives. Back to the beginning you go. You protect yourself with a machine gun and a shotgun. When the machine gun runs out of ammo, you reload it by shooting the shotgun. At least for me, surviving is typically my highest priority, but there are ways to maximize your score. For example, using the shotgun to kill enemies gives you more points. Killing multiple enemies in one shotgun blast scores you a multiplier. There are even more ways to discover as you play.

Where the game falters the most is also its most unique mechanism: the upgrade system. Killing enemies in certain ways will add to a counter. Once you kill enough enemies in that specific way, you will unlock a power up permanently for that run. For example, one of the easiest power ups is obtained by killing enemies with ricocheted shotgun pellets off the wall. Kill enough enemies in this manner and now your shotgun blasts split after connecting with an enemy. There are various other upgrades to obtain that all have their unique power. It''s up to you to discover them and part of the strategy of the game is obtaining these upgrades while trying to survive and increase your score.

While this sounds like a neat system, it''s the one thing keeping me from wanting to pick up Tormentor X Punisher over and over. This game is hard. You will die a lot. When you die, you have to start back and square one and unlock the power ups over again. At the beginning of the game, demons trickle in at you in small groups. There''s really only two viable power ups you can attempt to obtain during this phase. Because of how difficult the game is, you will play this phase over and over and over. It becomes so tedious. I want my arcade score chasers to ramp up quickly. I don''t want to spend a minute or two at the beginning of every run doing the same thing. I want to be running, gunning, and having a blast.

It''s for this reason I find playing Tormentor X Punisher to be tedious. Maybe if you''re a better player that can reliably survive longer than I can, you may enjoy your runs more, but as someone that sucks, you end up playing the same tedious beginning too much.

There are other negatives as well. The game is butt ugly. I looked over this game several times before buying it just because of how ugly it is. I caved in an got it after seeing some positive reviews. Along with its ugliness, everything is very muddles. It''s all dark browns and reds. On top of this, the game has tons of screen shake. Luckily, they''ve added an option to reduce (not remove) the screen shake, but the default option is so atrocious I don''t know how it released in that state. Clarity is big problem once enemies start dying en masse. The ground is littered with body parts and blood, further obscuring the red and brown enemies that clutter the screen. Good luck actually trying to see some of the smaller bosses. They get lost in the crowd. It would have been nice if they had highlighted the bosses or something to make them stand out more.

The main character is also incredibly foul mouthed. I get it, the game is going for an over the top brutal style. It doesn''t offend me, it just gets real tiring hearing her scream "**** YOU!!!" for the millionth time.

Overall, Tormentor X Punisher commits two big cardinal sins for a game of this genre. It''s tedious to restart a run and unlock power ups, which you will do often, and it has some severe clarity issues. I can''t recommend the game because of this.'),
