	 ('186125940','76561197997477460','3377690','20251230',6,0,0,0,8,'Amusingly, while I''m playing this I''m thinking it looks and plays like a clunky homebrew shmup like you''d have seen on an ''80s computer system like MSX or Atari 800. I finish the game, read the store description in preparation to write a quick review, and apparently this is a (very) minor remake of a clunky homebrew shmup on the PC-8800 from some indeterminate date in the 1980s. So close!

But looking up videos of that game, while the sound isn''t quite as good as it is here, your ship in the PC-8800 version seems to move faster, which is what really makes this already humdrum shmup a bit of a chore to play in this Steam version -- your ship is just too slow. There''s no intensity, everything seems stuck in 2nd gear.

So this is kind of neat as an extremely obscure piece of video game history (they don''t get much more obscure - I can''t find info on the original game anywhere other than one Youtube video), it''s not very fun to play. Worth the buck for video game and/or shoot ''em up historians, probably not to anyone else. 6/10'),
	 ('1110782','76561197986992213','234710','20251230',29,1,0,0,10,'Poker Night 2 is the sequel to Poker Night at the Inventory. It continues bringing together many popular characters from different video games. This time Poker Night features Claptrap (Borderlands 2), Brock Samson (The Venture Bros.), Ash (The Evil Dead), Sam (Sam and Max series) and GLaDOS (Portal) as your dealer.
In Poker Night 2 you have the option of playing either Texas hold ''em or Omaha hold ''em. In either case, tournaments start with a $20,000 buy-in.
An humorous opening sequence leads you down into the Inventory and introduces the characters. These characters will have fun lines to say on their own, but the real treat is when two or more characters talk with each other.
Each character also has a special item that you can win from them that unlocks items in Borderlands 2 and Team Fortress 2.
Like in Poker Night at the Inventory the sequel has no multiplayer option, so you always play against the same opponents.
The audio gags and discussions are amusing the first time you hear them, but they get annoying when you start hearing the same ones over and over again from one tournament to the next.
All in all, if you are looking for a good poker game, then you should try Poker Night 2. The funny conversations and the items you can win make this a fantastic little game.

Sounds 7/10
Graphics 8/10
Gameplay 7/10
Atmosphere 8/10

