	 ('71280589','76561198035991719','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,3,'Best game 15 out of 10. Play poker with Brock Sampson from Venture Bros, Ash from Ash vs the Evil dead, Claptrap from Borderlands, and Sam from Sam and Max.'),
	 ('21892491','76561198018399608','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'Telltale returns with their third poker game. This time it has Brock from Venture Bros, Claptrap from Borderlands, Evil Dead''s Ash Williams, Telltale''s own Sam and Max (originally of Lucas Arts fame), and finally GladOS from Portal as the dealer.

The Bruce Campbell impersonator of Ash feels a little underwhelming, and I wish they could have just got Bruce himself in for the role, but all the other characters are spot on. The dialogue is charming. The gaming and betting AI is much improved from the predecessor, and the Animation is much smoother. I also like how the different styles of animation are maintained in the same engine. When you see the different kinds of cell shading on Brock and Claptrap, while having a different style of 3D renduring on Ash, GladOS, and Sam and Max while all staying true to the original styles the characters came from, it is pretty amazing.

The only gripe I have is it seems the bluff vs tell animations are at random now, and there really is no longer a way to try and get a read on the characters, even after you buy them alcohol. In that way alone it feels like that is a bit of a downgrade from the previous Poker Night. Every other front seems to have improved though. They have now included Omaha as well as Texas Hold''em.  There is  now more diversified gaming AIs between the characters. For instance, Brock and Ash will constantly get into bidding wars, that everyone else should steer clear of. Finally, unlocking different table, card, and chip sets, also change the decor of the room, as well as add new themed dialogue to the characters and new elimination scenarios, which gives the game a little more longevity and adds to the fun.

The cross-game unlockables are kinda meh-ish, but hopefully with so much other stuff in both Team Fortress and Borderlands, that''s not the reason you''re playing. I still recommend getting this game on it''s own merits, and hope that Telltale revisits this franchise in the future, and maybe next time with a rotating cast of more characters.'),
