	 ('41146354','76561198027881741','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'Poker Night at the Inventory is the sequel to a simple poker game by Telltale Games. Now busy with being big shot "adventure" game makers, it''s unlikely they''ll ever release #3, but thankfully, what we''ve gotten is a fun game you can pick up or walk away from at any time.

Having not played some of the original poker night, I''m not entirely sure how the chemistry of that game was between characters, since it would seem with such different voices as Max, the Heavy and Strongbad, that the banter between everyone wouldn''t be all that great.

However, for Poker night 2, they switch up the roster, so the 4 characters this time are Brock Samson from the Venture Brothers, Claptrap from Borderlands 2, Ash from the Evil Dead franchise, and Sam from the Freelance Police franchise. What''s nice about their dialog is that they have some very nice banter with each other. Ranging from Max''s sincerity to Brock''s deadpan, to Claptrap''s snark, and Ash''s playfulness. However there''s one other player in the mix that definitely puts it above the previous game: your dealer is GLADOS from Portal. With her robotic deadpan humor injected into each hand, you''re sure to be amused as you make your way through each difficulty.

Admittedly, once you use all the tokens and complete all your challenges, the game is going to wear out its welcome as you struggle to get that final badge of 1 million lifetime, but in the time in between, there''s actually a lot of unique spins to be had. Using your tokens you win each round, you can unlock different playing fields in the house. One for each of the characters. So they can range from ambiance for a Pandora bar, a moolit bar celebrating Sam and Max''s 25th anniversary, a mad scientist layer for the venture brothers, a cemetary for Ash, or some high tech gizmos for Portal.

In each field, there are different dialogs unlocked, each one tailored for the character who has the "home field advantage". So for example, on ash''s field, they''ll talk about how Ash is secretly Brock''s Ancestor. Or on Sam''s field they''ll sing the Sam and Max Theme together. This cane give you insentive to try different fields as often as possible.

But the best part of the ambiance is the variety of elliminations. While each player has a set of stock ellimination phrases, on the unlocked fields, there will be one unique ellimination per round. So you could get shot with a confetti bazooka, attacked by Max, teleported away by Glados or Dr. Venture, or my favorite, have a loser eaten by the necrinomicon once Max says it incorrectly.

Gameplay itself is simple poker. You can choose 2 styles: Hold em with 2 cards, or Omaha with 4. While slightly disappointing there aren''t any other modes to use like say..seven stud, it works.

Overall, while the gameplay is nothing too special, except maybe to see if you can trick the computers with outrageous bluffing (you can..sometimes), it''s still a fun game that''s relatively quick to pick up, and can be continued for as long or as short as you want. Definitely give it a purchase. After all, it''s still cheaper than a triple a, and might last just as long.'),
