	 ('201461385','76561198389351262','3563710','20251230',1,0,0,0,0,'This is a critical review. Only giving it a thumbs up because the game is worth $3.00 USD.

I played the game for 3 in-game days. The first day I recall something about ''making armor and weapons in preparation.'' This doesn''t even seem to be applicable with the constant appearance of clients. Yes, clients can be ignored, and this could solve the issue. But if I''m ignoring the customers to make stuff for the customers, then I''m just wasting time.

The gameplay itself isn''t that great either. Step 1, require resources from outside. You can only hold so much, so there is a little shelf in the smith area to place bought items on. Step 2, go to the forge and pick the item you want to forge. To forge the item, you just tap space at the right time. Hammering is done as your press space, the weapon magically quenches and sharpens itself. Step 3, get the item from the anvil table thing behind you and give it to the customer.

The above is the entire game, just repeated with extra steps depending on the orders placed. Although you can use EXP to unlock new items to craft, it''s the same steps just longer. 

The inability to make use of the weapon, tool, and armor stands due to constant customers is frustrating. It''s a good mechanic that was wasted. The modeling for the game is great, the texturing is alright, the animation and scripting are somewhat poor.

Some things I would change about the game to make it better; Add a time of day where customers just do not show up. This allows players to make use of the prepared stations. Improve forging, smelting the metal, casting it, sharpening or hammering it, quenching, etc. Just something other than space bar on a green line. The leaning forward during movement isn''t necessary, and I have miss-clicked things due to stopping and clicking at the same time when the camera moves back.'),
