	 ('32163365','76561198139818199','500670','20251230',6,0,0,0,0,'Definitely a great game. The gameplay is really crazy and chaotic, but that''s the whole point. Still haven''t figured out how to kill some of the bosses & how to get the powerups, but the game makes me feel an urge to do it. You can get tired of that kind of gameplay, but again, that''s not a complaint  -- It''s really fun to play a round or two in a break. The ending goblin is hillarious, but could be even better. The shaking of the screen adds more madness to the atmosphere of the game, but I can understand all the nausea complaints, ''cause sometimes you really just loose the track of what''s going on in the arena. And I really hate the shielders. U guys rock. Thank you for the game.'),
	 ('20858872','76561198008322910','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'Initially the first 4 tournaments were fair and actually really fun. The dialogue was entertaining, the "showdown flops" were exciting, and generally decent. It is, however, when one reaches tournament #5 that the game decides to bend you over and show you who''s boss. An example: If you have two pair and the AI you''re in a showdown with has a only a pair, the game will make either the second-to-last card or the very last card coincide with the AI''s pair, making him have a 3-of-a-kind. Thus converting your easy win of "90%-chance of succeeding" to literally jackshit. This is just ONE example. 

If by chance you are one of only two people left at the end of the tournament and have twice or three-times as much chips as your last opponent, the game will give the AI consecutive wins and before you know it the game basically stole your win from you. These occurrances happen constantly from tournament #5 onwards. Also, bluffing for some reason becomes broken, where whenever you bluff, the majority of the AI have a strong hand. Whenever you have a good hand, the AI go "All-In" and scare you out. Plus, most of the time it was a bluff on their part.

All in all this game sucks you in thinking its fun and fair, then traps you in a never ending vicious cycle of rigged gameplay, ever making you hell-bent on winning at least ONE game and then stopping. But that''ll never happen.'),
	 ('135243431','76561198345355307','3330','20251230',1,0,0,0,10,'This is a great game for fiends that enjoy busting balls with the best of ''em!'),
	 ('74680332','76561198004546096','500670','20251230',5,3,0,0,0,'[b]Pros:[/b]
+ Very, very stylish - rip and tear while yelling obscenery
+ Good core mechanics (controls, movement, aiming etc)
+ Reload primary weapon by firing secondary ( ಠ_ಠ)

[b]Cons:[/b]
- Permanent death with no persistent upgrades (death = lose ALL progress)
- To make it worse, instant death to a single hit from anything
- Unnecessarily difficult and extremely repetitive 
- Upgrade system really sucks and takes the fun away
- No pause button, no save/load, no online coop without controllers

The game seems enjoyable at the beginning until you realize that a single hit from any enemy means instant death and loss of all progress. You retain nothing from your runs - no upgrades, no playable characters, nothing.

About two minutes into the run things suddenly get way too difficult and confusing. You end up restarting the game over and over and over again countless times gaining very little skill or new knowledge from each run. Describing this process as repetitive would be an underestimation.

The nail in the coffin is that you get upgrades by doing things like "kill an enemy with a blazing shotgun pellet by shooting the burning trail of the last bullet in the machinegun magazine". Sounds complex enough to you yet? Keep in mind that the game is very fast-paced and there''s a LOT going on on your screen at any moment. And you have to fulfill each upgrade requirement EIGHT TIMES. Oh, and yes, if you die, you have to start a new run and grind each upgrade all over again.

Why on earth the developers decided to take a gory, stylish game that looks like mindless fun and turn it into a horrible, difficult, complicated, unrewarding, and repetitive grind is simply beyond me. Do not buy.'),
