	 ('108015467','76561198126877995','1661180','20251230',1,0,0,0,10,'Your mission is to repair signaling devices to communicate with an alien species. To do that, you need to locate and extract specific resources to repair the device. To do that, you need to extract and sell a variety of other resources, which you use to build equipment and fund travel to neighboring solar systems to find more resources. Thus, you explore and hop from system to system and planet to planet, finding resources to extract to complete your mission.

I think Signals is a novel and interesting take on the trading / exploration / mining genre of games. I like that it''s casual and that you can complete a game in an hour. There''s a set of six scenarios with different objectives, a weekly challenge, and an open-ended run mode with a uniquely generated system each time you play. The game play is simple but engaging, the graphics are attractive, and the soundtrack is good.

There are some bugs and quirks. The fonts of the planet names, in both the Planetarium and menus for planets in the system map, are squashed and illegible on my 1600 x 900 resolution laptop. Changing the resolution in the settings either does nothing, or messes up the interface in other ways. When I completed the challenge, I received the Steam reward for completing the Run mode (not for completing the challenge). When I complete a Run mode, my score is never recorded on the leader board.

While I like the casual nature of the game, it would be nice if it was a bit more challenging (with some possibility that you could lose). And better endings - you should at least get some summary stats about how many minerals you extracted, income you generated, worlds you explored, etc. All that being said, I really enjoy the game and it''s reasonably priced. I''m glad to support these new developers in their first game, and think a few bug fixes and some basic additions would add significantly to the replay value.'),
