	 ('80574868','76561198108149142','1197770','20251230',3,0,0,0,10,'Great fun! I enjoyed this far more than i expected. the voice acting and hilarious dialog are fantastic, and the body physics and textures are top notch. I also enjoyed the colorful and artistic design and lighting effects, which create a surreal fairy tale atmosphere. Mia Sloane''s voice acting and ...ahem... "simulated pleasure", are the real gem here. she really sells it and makes the rather repetitive journey quite enjoyable. to be fair, the combat system and gameplay mechanics are very basic, and the animations can be a tad sketchy, as well as  being a relatively short campaign. however, the artistic beauty of the game, its unique quirkiness, and highly erotic sex scenes are definitely worth the price tag. yes, it could use some improvements, but for an indie game budget, its pretty damn good. I hope to see an expanded universe with Lilly.'),
	 ('9057912','76561197961175535','234710','20251230',2,0,0,0,2,'I have to believe there are better poker games out there, though for how cheap it was I guess I can''t complain too much.  But I will anyway.

I just want to take the developers by their shoulders and shake them violently until they come to their senses.  The simplest changes would improve it dramatically.  As it is, the canned diologue gets stale fast.  I turned it off after a couple games, but now I have to watch them miming their lines.  That''s right - you can''t skip the incessant dialogue.  It turns fifteen minute games into thirty minute games.  It turns thirty minute games into purgatory.  Then there''s the menu - even the *menu screens* take forever.  There''s like a four second delay to move between them.  And when you press escape, it likes to default not to the actual menu, but to the unlocks screen.  Why?  Because fuck you that''s why.  I can only recommend this game to Buddhist monks for whom limitless patience is a possibility.  

But if you find it for $2 then, meh, it can mix up your game library when other games get stale and you forget how much of your time Poker Night 2 loves wasting.  But don''t worry.  It''ll remind you how agonizingly time-wasting it is all over again, and you''ll wind up writing a review to vent your frustrations.'),
