	 ('42087568','76561198046193538','840930','20251230',2,0,0,0,10,'Bing Bong XL is a score-attack arcade-like game that can be played with merely one button, falling into the category of "easy to learn, difficult to master". The gameplay feels similar to one of NIckervision''s other titles, [url=https://store.steampowered.com/app/615610/]Orbt XL[/url], so if you enjoyed that then you''ll likely enjoy Bing Bong XL. On the main menu is the tutorial, which essentially is simply an infographic about the game''s core mechanics; It''s very straightforward, and if you get a game over you have the option to retry instantly, upload your score to twitter, view the leaderboards, or simply go back to the main menu. Overall Bing Bong XL serves as a good timekilling minigame, assuming you don''t get addicted to it.

As I stated earlier, the gameplay is very similar to Orbt XL, except this time it follows a linear format instead of a polar one. You control a circle that moves back and forth between the top of the screen, scoring a point each time the orb touches the top or bottom of the screen while avoiding the obstacles that come at you from the right. You can also score additional points if you narrowly avoid an obstacle, or by collecting power-ups that occasionally appear. The tricky part about the game comes into how your circle is controlled; it is always moving, and your only control over the circle is to slow it down by holding down the button (Spacebar by default), essentially giving it two speeds to move at.

The game has adjustable resolutions, fullscreen toggle, vsync and controller support, complete with optional controller vibration. There are also adjustable audio and music sliders should you wish to make one louder than the other, or mute one or both of them entirely in favor of your own music. You can also change the controls, allowing you to press whichever button you want if for some reason you don''t want to press the spacebar or the A-button on your controller, or your left mouse button for gameplay.

I recommend Bing Bong XL because of its simplicity and replayability; in a similar vein to Nickervision''s other games, you can unlock more "characters" to play as by simply playing the game more, as each point you score gets you closer and closer to unlocking something. It''s easily worth the $1 USD price tag, especially if it goes on any kind of sale.'),
	 ('8472209','76561198044064309','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'So, the not recommended for integrated intel is serious business.  I loved the game while worked, and the TF2 items are sweet, but it just stopped working one day.  Tried re-installing, etc.  The tech support forum, as well as that adendum (don''t think the non-recommendation was there during the summer sale) suggests that they''ve no intention of fixing it.  At the price it''s selling at, I can''t really blame them.  Still, I recommend the game for anyone that doesn''t fall into the same category as me, but likes TF2 or the previous Poker Night, or the characters in the current one.'),
