	 ('202115336','76561198083376855','2427430','20251230',6,0,0,0,10,'[i][b]Come in! Don''t stand there![/b][/i]

I’m not exactly a big connoisseur of the series, and I had never played [i]Clear Sky[/i] or [i]Call of Pripyat[/i].

Back in the day, I never managed to get my hands on copies of those games, and over time, my only experience with the original trilogy boiled down to just a handful of playthroughs in [i]Shadow of Chernobyl[/i] without mods.

In other words, even though I’ve done some research on the accusations of cut content this version of the game has received, firsthand I have no idea what the differences are or what was supposedly lost. I also have no clue what a heavily modded version of the game is like—and I don’t think that should be taken into account anyway.

I’m also aware of the technical issues this edition had at launch, and I’ll say this right away: in its current version (1.2), the game works more or less as it should (with all the jank, bugs and such). And visually, using native FSR with Sharpness maxed out, the graphics are more than acceptable—though still far from being a genuine remaster.

I spent a few hours on the original version to get a sense of the difference, and frankly, if you want a clean experience, the advantages of benefiting from an updated engine running flawlessly on modern machines—plus the addition of some excellent new rendering effects—make this the ideal way to play the game unmodded. It’s night and day.

That said…

[i]Call of Pripyat[/i] is [b]almost[/b] the definitive version of GSC Game World’s surreal, open-ended, radioactive adventures—in all their vodka-infused, Soviet depression glory.

It seems to me this was an attempt to expand and consolidate the formula of the first two games, keeping and reinforcing most of their best elements while discarding what didn’t work so well or didn’t show much promise of becoming truly interesting.

Just notice how much better and more natural the game’s pacing and progression are compared to [i]Clear Sky[/i], and you’ll see that embracing the more exploration- and narrative-driven nature of SoC—instead of intrusive faction wars—was the right call.

The game flows so much better there’s not even a debate.

This resulted in a more consistent and functional experience than the previous games. Although, I should add that it never quite reaches very high highs. It’s precise in what it wants to be, and works far better than anyone might expect—but it doesn’t have as much personality.

Retaining the essence of SoC''s exploratory experience, with realistic geometry, scale, and intuitive navigation, they dropped the small semi-open maps in favor of three modestly sized maps with an even more liberating, immersive, and interconnected feel.

Starting the game with open objectives scattered across different locations, they doubled down on giving the player more freedom—constantly teasing your curiosity with many points of interest scattered across the horizon, inviting you to decide where to go and what to do in an organic way, without abandoning a certain degree of friendly support for the adventure.

Just like in SoC, they point you in the right direction, but now you could say the overall quality of the tasks you perform has been diversified and better disguised so they never feel intrusive.

These spaces are a masterclass in open-world design—built with a memorably intuitive layout, on a scale that simultaneously makes you feel you’re in a vast place, but small and distinct enough for you to learn how to navigate and orient yourself without constantly checking the map.

The hubs in the game—much smaller, with well-organized NPCs—are also conveniently placed near the center, always appearing invitingly along whichever route you take.

There’s also been a slight expansion in inventory and item variety, with new weapons and armor (including the addition of a helmet category), consumables that provide more varied buffs (which unfortunately aren’t that useful outside anomaly areas), and the ability to set shortcuts for anything.

Weapons have been rebalanced, retaining the nuances between them while keeping most of them viable for long periods of time (pistols included).

Quests, while not impressive by today’s standards, are much more elaborate and better paced—some with multiple stages and different ways to complete them, and with repercussions that lead to other quests or at least get addressed in the game’s epilogue, like a classic 90s RPG.

Needless to say, the game overflows with a dark and immensely captivating atmosphere, with explosive, noisy contrasts alongside brooding, contemplative ones. It’s refreshing to play a game comfortable with letting the player soak in the silence and monotony— inherent to the realistic redundancy that emerges from a surreal, post-apocalyptic setting like this. It’s a feeling of wild isolation, of being in a vast, practically abandoned place, combined with a constant unease that something is always lurking.

Finding a new and mysterious location in this game is all the more alluring.

However, all this freedom came at the cost of a more structured sense of progression.

While [i]Shadow of Chernobyl[/i], despite its sharp late-game difficulty spike, has a constant sense of progression that gets better and more terrifying the closer you get to the end—with enemies becoming stronger and drastically more numerous—[i]Call of Pripyat[/i]’s difficulty curve feels rather plain and disconnected, with only a handful of scripted sequences providing any real sense of challenge.

As a result, the journey to the ending feels lukewarm—though you do get a few highlights, such as the trek to Pripyat and the X8 lab. Instead of one last grand push into the heart of darkness—with warring factions, explosions everywhere, and a grueling dash through the radiation-soaked corridors inside the Chernobyl sarcophagus—the game simply ends, leaving little lasting impression of the mission you were there to accomplish.

Combat is as crunchy and satisfying as ever, but fights against humans have become far less frequent compared to the first two games. The looser map structure wasn’t put to great use, as most of the locations you visit don’t have planned encounters. Consequently, outside of quests, you rarely run into enemies that aren’t mutants.

Even bandits, who are quite rare, mostly leave you alone.

As good as it is, the world needed more dynamism to generate emerging situations that would compensate for the lack of more pre-planned events.

Finding new items while exploring also seems neglected, with a very low incidence of useful loot (aside from ammo and such) appearing as you progress. Perhaps they chose not to scatter too many powerful items around and risk breaking the game’s balance—something that was less of a concern in the original, given its slightly more rigid exploration structure. Still, even though you technically have more types of things to find, it feels like you’re always forced to buy what you need.

Artifacts are more reliably obtainable and valuable, but their usability has been greatly reduced, with their slots tied to the armor you’re wearing—which, in most cases, doesn’t have enough slots to make a decent combination. This is made worse by the fact that almost all of them, even the weakest, cause radiation—making it practically mandatory to dedicate at least one slot to an anti-radiation artifact.

Still... Despite everything not clicking as well as I’d like—and resulting in a slightly less memorable experience—the quality of the game speaks for itself, in deeply engaging and immersive tones. [i]Call of Pripyat[/i] is a clear refinement of the best ideas presented in the earlier games, and even if a few elements were lost along the way, I could never condemn a game for being “just excellent”.

It’s a great game—not too big, not too small—and it has an excellent pace. Definitely worth your time.'),
