	 ('174854341','76561198028767808','3009850','20251230',23,0,0,0,9,'I like it.
But! If you want something more prison focused, the original Hard Time may be a better choice for you.

This iteration has a broader focus, as you are able to be a prison guard or regular citizen in addition to the usual prisoner. After your sentence ends you are free to roam the streets, and even become a guard too.
Because of this, it has seemingly limited the prisoner gameplay. Not enough to make me dislike the game, as the new areas and roles are certainly an enjoyable addition. 
It''s different in scope to the old games, and ultimately, that is fine. 

The gameplay and setting is the standard fare for an M Dickie game. If you''ve played one of his games before you''ll know what to expect. If you haven''t, then try to accept non standard control schemes. An unexpectedly in-depth wrestling/fighting system. Goofy and irrational AI. Outdated graphics. Distinct and non copyright infringing characters. Repetitive music. 
It''s glorious.

Steam Workshop is now available. That is always nice.'),
	 ('174672551','76561198152109749','3009850','20251230',1,0,0,0,10,'Played the first Hard Time as a kid on Windows XP, then played the 2D mobile version on my old Samsung phone. Now, playing Hard Time III. and it is still another great gem from Mat Dickie!'),
	 ('19284949','76561198000328048','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'This game provided me with countless hours of entertainment, and likely will continue to do so (at least until I get the last achievement I''m after). Even then, I can see myself coming back to it in the future as a wonderful relaxation game for multiple reasons:

1) The character banter. After investing as many hours in this as I did in the first 48-72 hours post purchasing Fallout 4, the character banter does get repetitive. However, it never ceases to amuse me, somehow. The writing and wit are cleverly done, and switching themes with the decks/chips/tables combos creates entirely new endings when you bust someone out.

2) The themes. As mentioned above, applying a full set (deck, chips, table) of game-specific features changes the entire playing field - literally. The entire Inventory room gets bedazzled, and one of the characters gets a brand new look. I can''t say for sure, but it also seems like the banter changes slightly sometimes with it.

3.) The gameplay itself. There''s two modes - Texas Hold Em'' and Omaha Hold Em''. Omaha starts with 4 cards, requiring 2 from said cards and 3 from the drawn cards to complete your ''hand'', while Texas of course starts with 2 and combines any best 3 cards. I love and prefer Omaha by leaps and bounds. It significantly increases your chances of winning, as well as helps you make better decisions about when to play and when to fold.

All of that said, there IS something I wish this game had that Poker Night 1 did but this does not, and that''s a difficulty setting. I would like to increase the difficulty at this point, as it''s a rather fun game and would probably extend its lifespan by double for someone like me.

Telltale, anyone there, if you see this, screw my comment above about difficulty and please bring us Poker Night 3! These games are time wasting crack and I need more of my fix!

Yes, you should by this game. Especially if you''re seeing this at the $1.99 Thanksgiving sale price, or Christmas sale later on, or any sale ever. And even if not, for $5 it''s more than worth it. I''ve gotten less play out of $40-$50 games, and that''s saying something, hopefully.'),
