	 ('42525126','76561197971598230','452410','20251230',20,0,0,0,10,'This is hard because I really want to like this game. In fact, there is a lot to love. 
Lets start with the love!  
First and foremost, the art is gorgeous. I really dig the style, the animations, and the settings. Its consistent, its quality, its badass.

The player controller, besides some strange design choices, is very solid indeed. These controls are hampered by those design choices, but is overall, tight, responsive, and feels good.

The enemy designs are interesting, a little cookie cutter (nothing wrong there), and will come at you in different ways. Perfect, were still running strong.

So what are the cons for me... everything is subjective, and this may simply ''not be my kind of game'' but it feels like this game wants to desperately be too many things.
More than anything, it feels like this game wants to be Metal Slug with ultra arcadey action, but it wants to be Super Meat with the quick deaths and repeats, but lets throw in some Mark of Ninja that makes us really think out our moves and analyse the best way to progress...
Oh also... lets throw some QTE''s in there.

Its confusing, because most of these disparate game mechanics are not properly explained. Its trial and error, and the solutions are discovered easily enough, but half of the game wants you to run fast, the other half wants you to stop and do a tired and very basic QTE. The QTE''s are all based off the same principal, and in most cases all it does is hinder the second to second gameplay. 
The worst offender being the time bomb, which you too often trigger in passing and have to turn around to go back to, stalling again. Why not have these in the middle of infinite spawners/the energy source for a enemy with shields until you take it out in a well defended area/ANYTHING. All the QTE''s seem to do is frustrate.

Next you have the noise, firstly, the score pops into the air and falls down becoming debris... are we arcade or not? It''s too much. It is really hard to keep track of where stuff is, and everything seems to be in the foreground. 
Heres an example:
There is an enemy, a coffin, and a hostage all on top of eachother. Which gets hit when I shoot? Oops, it was the hostage.

I get you want barriers, but it just doesnt make sense. The coffins look like they are in the background, everyone walks in front of them, yet you will hit them and not the enemy behind it, then that enemy kills me with a single shot kill? I mean... gah. Ya got me... but not in a way that feels good. 
It is EXACTLY the same when you hit a hostage in the same scenario. I get it, risk reward, but why not strap that time bomb thats just being an bore TO THE HOSTAGE. It could be counting down from level start, or when you trigger. 
Or maybe the VAMPIRES suck the hostages blood if you hit them.
OR the vampires are moving towards a group of hostages.

I know Im being that person, the one who in hindsight is sayin ''Oh why didnt you do this'', but its because for the most part I cannot criticise this. 
This is great polished work, and there dont seem to be technical issues or bugs. This is obviously made with love and passion, and it shows. For my money, and once again, my tastes the major issue is one of design.
Its just not that fun at this time.

It is also very hard for me to care for the characters or setting, I am in no way invested. If this was a pick up run and gun arcade fest, cool. But is it? I dont know... because what I was expecting was some kind of story line, and maybe thats coming but the ''Campaign'' really feels and is presented like a bunch of game types. Yes it gets tougher, but there is really no sense of progess.
Each level sees you doing the same bunch of QTE''s with a side of action, then you get your score, then you go to another location. Maybe there is a proper story coming, perhaps Im missing something.

The game touts itself as being fast paced (unless you are QTE''ing or accidentally shooting scenery), arcadey (except when its constantly not), single player game (yes), set in a cartoon world (that I know nothing about), with a pumping synth soundtrack (the music is rad as heck).

Lets say I made you a milkshake. I said ''Yo, you want a strawberry milkshake?'' and you would say "Heck yes!".
Then I give you something that looks like a strawberry milshake, and for a second it tastes like a strawberry milkshake, but then you taste kimchi, and when you take a second look someone has garnished it with a bacon.
You, like me, may love all those things... actually this metaphor may be bunk, that MIGHT be incredible. But its probably not.

TLDR: Do I recommend this game?
This is tough, because like I said, theres nothing technically wrong with it. I want to say maybe its just not to my tastes, but I have some diverse AF tastes in games.
Im ultimately thumbing this up, because it does show a lot of promise and I really want to see it succeed, and to other tastes might be perfection. Also its in early access and dropped only early this year.

It is obvious that the devs are talented, and committed, that much is easy to see, so I have faith we are gonna see a lot of good things coming from the devs.'),
