	 ('84667641','76561198354553719','386990','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'I''m pleased to say that I enjoyed this Visual Novel. However, it''s not without faults to say the least.

It uses too many similes throughout the text for my taste. Sometimes it''s nice to have some, but there aren''t just a few to “spice things up,” so to speak. There''s almost an overwhelming amount. The description I enjoyed the most were the ones that wasn''t a simile, mostly because it [i] was [/i] so overused.

Too repetitive with certain words. The words that come to mind the most are “had been,” two verbs that''s better to avoid as much as possible. I found a number of times when these two were used twice in one paragraph, sometimes even thrice. You might have to rewrite the entire sentence, but it [i] is [/i] possible to not use them.
Samantha''s name also comes up quite often even when it''s just her. If there''s another person with her, then I can understand the usage of her name more than when she''s alone. Because when she''s by herself and you''ve used her name once, you need only use pronouns because we''ve already gathered that''s her name.

An enormous amount of commas. Some sentences made sense for them to have their commas here and there, but other ones—like the longer string of words—could''ve done without the amount. I''m a comma addict myself—which I can only assume whoever worked on the text is based on said thing—and it''s hard not to add one when it would work in your mind but is necessarily not needed. There were many instances where the sentence would still flow well if that pause hadn''t even been there.

Alright, that''s it for my constructive criticism, now onto the things I liked!

Despite the things that I mentioned previously, the text wasn''t too bad. It had character, delightful dialogue, and a good use on the empathising of certain words. I only really found around twenty errors within the span of eight hours, which is not bad. Now, these errors are anything that''s worded incorrectly, words that make no sense in the description, letters missing, etc. This can be overlooked depending on how often it happens, and since I didn''t quit the game, let''s say that it didn''t bother me too much.

The art-style—though confusing at first as it makes the characters appear much younger than they actually are—is quite nice. It''s pleasing to the eyes with an abundance of colours that work well together. The backgrounds and overall scenery was certainly one of my favourite parts to look at.

The atmosphere is incredible. The added noises makes it feel like you''re actually there, and that you are Samantha, instead of just a player. The music is also beautiful. It really conveys the emotions for the scenes and whatever the characters are experiencing. 

Speaking of which, I absolutely adored the characters. They have very distinctive personalities almost to the point where you could write down their dialogues without their names being mentioned and still be able to identify on who is speaking. My favourite type of characters—as strange as it may be—are the ones who are rude, inconsiderate, sadistic, etc. They are just so fun to write!

And finally, to say that Asphyxia is emotional would be an understatement. It is positively heart-wrenching. I love how Samantha''s condition was portrayed. It''s almost nerve-racking on how accurate it is—at least, from my point of view. I have felt similar or much the same things that this character has gone through. And that makes me think that this game could be a teaching moment for those who don''t know or understand how it is or feels to have that condition. So if you have friends, family, or otherwise that want to know more about this one thing, then this could be an answer for them. And if it doesn''t work, at least you tried.'),
