	 ('39922641','76561198085352614','234710','20251230',4,1,0,0,10,'Poker Night 2: How to make your blood pressure go up.

Alright, you know EXACTLY why you''re playing this game. You don''t care about the previous game, you''re here for the 4 promotional hats for TF2, that''s around 95% of the actual purchase reasons for this. Poker Night 2 is a sequel that doesn''t really improve on the previous game by any real measurable standard. The basic poker gameplay is the same Texas Hold''Em except now you can play it Omaha style and thus lower your chance of not blowing 20k to virtual characters like the lonliest nerd in the dorm. Graphics are nice, the little customizable stuff are cool if a bit annoying in that you have to spend weird tokens to get them rather than just unlock them by beating the characters in question.

So why am I not reccomending a basic poker game?

The voice acting.

Oh I know, that''s pretty much the remaining 5% of why you''re buying this rather than play Prominence Poker which is free. You want to hear the sounds of Brock Sampson, Claptrap, Sam and Ash make banter while awkwardly staring at you like you just ripped a fart in the middle of prayer at a family dinner. But like many Telltale games, the funny lines get repeated over and over and over and over and over and over AND OVER AGAIN. It gets so repetative that it begins to make you mess up. You''re trying to play the game while you hear Ash ask about Sam''s DeSoto for the 12th time this specific session and you feel that someone in Telltale has heard of the "Salt and Pepper Diner" joke and is trying to replicate it on you. 

So, TL:DR should you buy this game? In all honesty no. The actual gameplay is alright and the jokes start off as funny, but if you intend to fight your way to all the TF2 hats you are in for a hellish ride of dry and unfunny banter from 4 different characters.'),
