	 ('195332895','76561197984638483','2427430','20251230',5,3,0,0,10,'TLDR: A great, albeit simple, enhanced edition. 

This game currently has a 40% mixed score. I think it''s way better than that, but for you to agree with me you need to share my taste and priorities. 

Many players don''t care about the optimisation and memory management of a game, they don''t notice a difference in details and micro details, both in terms of visuals and smoothness. They are looking for this to be a oblivion remastered. 

For me personally, i''m more of a fan of when a developer makes something HIGHLY efficient. So let''s start with something that is widely overlooked about this enhanced edition. The fluidity of the engine. The x-engine has always had huge memory management and frame pacing issues, some people notice these, some do not. I was always someone that noticed that the s.t.a.l.k.e.r games had incredibly bad microstuttering. In that sense, this enhanced edition mirrors my experience with skyrim special edition - just on a lower budget. 

It''s x65/dx12 and more optimised/streamlined nature makes the memory management of the game FAR FAR FAR better than before. So you can play this game with really consistent frametimes on the pc. The dx10/9 versions had lurches and stutters and all kinds of inconsistencies of smoothness. This does not have any of those problems. Which is more important to me than if it looked like oblivion remastered. Because it serves as an incredible base for modding and expansion. You need something that''s smooth as a foundation for modding to thrive. 

Skyrim special edition had DEEP levels of frame pacing fixes built into it from the outsourced devs that beth used at the time. And as a result the modding scene thrived with the engines new ability to maintain perceptual smoothness regardless of draw calls and asset streams. I just had a play session of this game, and the entire time i never had a single spike in the frametime graph. That''s incredible in this day in age. 

To me, that foundation makes this version of the game absolutely a VERY POSITIVE from me. That does not mean however that other people care about the same foundations as i do. 

There are faults, the game is setup to use FSR 3, which does take care of aliasing, but it''s not a great implementation. I personally used optiscaler to convert it to DLSS 4. And then i used sharpening on top of that. Which resulted in a very clean image, but the lack of upscalers means that most players won''t have that technical knowhow to even consider that a possibility if it''s not already built into the game,  also, the game''s upscaled textures don''t really enhance the details. The global illumination, while something I appreciate, is not the best implementation, and as a result it tends to flicker on objects and environments, which can create a pulsing effect. 

If you are playing this game without FSR enabled, please enable it at the very least, don''t play this with FXAA, it looks extremely blurry. 

Overall, it could use more work, but the smoothness and foundational optimisation is there. People are going to realise that eventually i believe.'),
