	 ('159934704','76561198141430579','234710','20251230',6,1,0,0,10,'Ill just copy and paste my review on the first game here:

Poker Night at the Inventory is just a simple poker game. It doesnt do anything different gameplay wise than any of your free poker games you can download.

But, what this game brings to the table is a world filled with humor and stories. This is what makes literal fake online gambling fun. The characters and the stories they tell. The free items also help with making it even better.

All in all, its a game I can recommend for anyone who even enjoys a single one of the characters! 

(There is more customization now so the game is immediately better)'),
	 ('64365991','76561197971430271','1148620','20251230',5,0,0,0,10,'Plunder is an action RPG that''s sort of a cross between of Sid Meier''s Pirates! and Dink Smallwood.  You sail around a fairly large map, have lite naval battles complete with boarding attacks, and land at various locations to explore towns, find side quests, and explore dungeons.  

There''s a pretty good variety in weapons, with each type also having a few unique variants that are worth finding for the novelty alone, like a bow that lets you steer the arrows after firing or a hand axe that heals you when you do damage, and even more build customization in the other equipment and stats.  The dungeon exploration is pretty engaging, as they all have distinct puzzles and riddles to access all the unique loot.  You can hire AI crew members to tag along behind you, and even unlock your own settlement that you can expand and improve with all the money you stop really needing otherwise.

There''s a few problems - items on the ground vanish if you leave a map, even ones that were hand-placed to appear the first time you enter, so if you go exploring and bail before completing an island or dungeon, you might not have the tools necessary to do so.  The storage is extremely limited, even if you take into account the stashes on your ship, and juggling weapons and items when you find something you want to take along is frustrating, especially if you''re trying to complete multiple quests that require delivery.  The ally AI makes recruitable crew mostly useless, and sometimes downright hazardous [your own musketeers tend to shoot you in the back of the head during ship boarding] and the final ship upgrade is unnecessary, extremely expensive, and makes you go painfully slowly.  It was always fun finding a new unique weapon or unlocking a new spell, but many of the spells are too situational given the extremely fast pace of the combat, and casting requires a free offhand that''s better used for a shield.  You can go fishing, but about half of the spots where fish appear are literally inaccessible with your hook.

Plunder is a great game that feels straight out of the era of Flash games that were more compelling than a lot of AAA titles of the time, with just enough to see and do to keep you hooked without becoming a slog.'),
	 ('49567118','76561198263499297','3330','20251230',4,0,0,0,10,'This is one of the best games I''ve ever played, and I think this is my most replayed game yet! I''m pretty sure I first played Zuma when I was 2-3 years old. The replay value isn''t the best best, but Gauntlet Mode has a lot of it! I also love getting a bunch of sick moves and stuff. 10/10, you should totally play this if you haven''t yet! :D

Edit: Also it may say I''ve only played this game for a short amount of time, but recently I bought it again on Steam because it''s just that good! (I owned it on Big Fish Games before.)

Edit from 2022 (changed a tiny bit in 2024): I''ve heard that the Zuma games may actually be clones of a different game, Puzzloop. I do still think Zuma is very fun but you should definitely check out Puzzloop 1 and 2.'),
