	 ('86927318','76561198180059954','386990','20251230',4,0,0,0,10,'HIGHLY RECOMMEND. I''ve read several Visual Novels on Steam, and although it is rather short, Asphyxia is one of the best.

My introduction to Ebi-Hime''s work was the even shorter novella, "Lily of the Valley" which I also greatly enjoyed. Ebi-Hime''s body of work is large, the complete bundle pack costs over $100. While browsing to see which title I might purchase next, I noticed Asphyxia''s description stated it was a reinventing of Romantic Era British Poets and Writers as high school girls.

I am a former English Professor and public school teacher so I immediately hit "buy", and I was not disappointed. As a literature nerd I was absolutely blown away by how well this little story captured the depth of intrigue, passion, and actual history between the writers of the Romantic Era. 

This is only the 2nd VN I''ve read from Ebi-Hime, so I can''t fairly say it''s "the best". But I doubt any of the other stories will impress me this much. The amount of historical non-fiction subtly packed into what amounts to a Shojo Manga short story, is a literary achievement. It makes me wish I was still teaching so I could recommend this story to my students. On the surface it''s a shallow drama about teen girls fighting about what girls fight over. However, it is an enjoyable, easy to understand way to access the historical interactions between famous writers of the Romantic Age. Well worth your time if you are a student (or a  Literature Geek, like me).

Knowing the history and works of these authors as I do, I LOL''d when "Georgia Byron & Percy Shelly" were introduced. The characterization of "Lady Byron" is laser accurate.  Ebi-Hime has clearly studied classic literature in great detail.

My only criticism: IMHO, Ebi-Hime''s books are a bit pricey considering how short they are.  The full price should be $2 cheaper. The first VN I ever read on Steam was "Cupid" which is extremely long, highly animated and scored, has many questions which branch the story, and is "Free to Play". Perhaps that has set the bar too high for all the other VN''s I have read and will read in the future.

Regardless of my one mild criticism, I enthusiastically recommend this title. I bought this on sale, but if I was still a teacher I would (grudgingly) pay double the full price because of its extreme value as educational entertainment.'),
