	 ('98300573','76561197998441405','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'It might seem odd to write an in-depth review of a game that is no longer available for purchase, but this isn''t a buyer''s guide. It''s for me, personally.
I happened to purchase this game Lord-knows-when and decided to play it in earnest only recently. As I''m very familiar with the first game, I had certain expectations I was hoping would be met. Unfortunately, they were not. This is my farewell to a defunct developer studio I would have loved to love.

Sequels very often go bigger and better. That''s just the nature of it, as a sequel comes with creator experience and audience expectations. It''s evident that this was the case for this particular sequel as well. Sadly the expansion this time around was almost solely focused on the spectacle.
It''s not unexpected. After all, what is there to improve about the mechanics of a poker game simulation? For this sequel specifically, that would be the indicators for the probability of victory during a showdown, when there is no more betting to be done and the table cards are flipped one after the other. There is also an almost hidden menu to buy drinks for your poker partners to loosen them up and increase the frequency of their subconscious tells.
A feature that is only explained immediately after using it.

The drinks are an excellent example of all the ways this game falls short. They''re purchased with a currency that''s earned by playing well during the poker tournaments. This means the player is expected to already play well in order to unlock a feature to lower the game''s difficulty; a paradoxical situation stacked against inexperienced players and made even worse with the lack of initial guidance. After all, the currency is also used to unlock cosmetic alterations to the playing table, explicitly made appealing by their collectability and the little changes to character dialogue. Unless the player happens to be curious, goes for the achievement or is faced with one of the game''s bounty challenges, they''ll never think to try buying drinks for their competitors, much less so in an attempt to make the game easier.

This blunder, perhaps innocently thought up just for the immersion, is telling. The same lack of care and attention to player experience shines through in so many more aspects of the game. For a game with such limited interactivity, there are stunningly many bugs. Audio cues that are unaffected by the volume settings, character animations breaking, incorrectly tallied pots, incorrectly calculated probabilities; nothing game breaking, but there are more than enough to leave a disappointing impression of amateur work.
And nothing could contrast the audiovisuals more. The bar is lovingly rendered in full 3D, and so is every character. They move in believable, fluid and (most of all) characterful animation, ranging from the subtle twitches of Ash''s prosthetic hand as he deliberates how to react to a high bet, to Brock punching the table in barely constrained fury when he loses a big wager. All this is set to tasteful background music and supported by stellar voice acting. And for every single action, there are reactions from all the players: Whether you fold, call or bet, whether you wager high or low, whether you call a bluff or walk into a trap, whether anyone unexpectedly wins or loses - there are fully realised conversations for anything and everything that happens during the match. Left to their own devices, the players will even start chatting among themselves about their respective backstories, adventures and hobbies. The craftsmanship that went into bringing these characters to life is stunning.
Which is what makes it all the more painful if within the span of minutes, a conversation repeats. The first time Sam explains how dogs don''t see in black and white, then snaps his fingers to let us experience the trippy green and red "dog vision", it''s amusing and cute. When it happens the third time in the same tournament, it has thoroughly lost its charm. And that''s only for the conversations that have charm to begin with.

My least favourite element of this game is Max. Not even an active player, he''s a returning character from the first game, relegated to a supporting role for Sam. Most often he''ll chat back and forth with his buddy or fetch his chips for him if Sam wins big, but for some conversations, he''ll run around the table shooting his gun or float across the room posessed by some demonic entity. Every time this happens, I am immediately yanked out of the atmosphere of a cozy poker bar. Max isn''t even the only one who does this - both Reginald van Winslow, the Inventory''s host, and GLaDOS, the dealer, will merrily move right in front of the table and up to me, destroying any illusion of me physically sitting at the table with my fellow contestants. And just to make absolutely certain I feel as unwelcome as I could feel, none of the characters ever express joy over my presence. To the contrary; both Max and Reginald, who''d welcomed and complimented me in the first game, will outright question my presence and my choices. That is, when Max isn''t busy saying to my face that I don''t have any friends.
I''ve danced around the comparisons between the first game and this one long enough, and I hope it doesn''t come as a surprise at this point that I far prefer the predecessor. While nearly identical in execution, the atmosphere is vastly different. The characters are just as quirky and well-realised, and on top of occasionally taunting and mocking the player, they stay classy and don''t ever resort to insults. Furthermore they can swallow their envy long enough to compliment me on a strong hand or a good play.
In the first game, I feel like I''m in a bar, sitting at a poker table with four wildly wacky characters. With Poker Night 2, I feel like I''m trapped with six stand-up comedians pulling off all their routines with a poker game as set dressing.

Admittedly the first game could get similarly overbearing with time. After all, there are only so many interactions; sooner or later you will see repeats. There is a reason why I don''t list that as a negative against the first game, however. That one has a setting in the options that determines how often the characters chat, going from non-stop conversation to only announcing their moves. On top of that you can right click at any time to skip parts of the ongoing conversation, so if there happens to be some exchange you don''t feel like hearing, you can simply end it. Both those features are conspicuously absent here. Not only that; conversations also tend to be more elaborate, with long bits of chatting during which no player will make a move. Thus the game will irregularly, arbitrarily and unavoidably take control away from the player, and you can''t even alt+tab away because the game will pause while you''re doing anything else.

All those issues remind me of another of Telltale Games'' series: The Walking Dead. The first season was a lovely, self-contained story about a father and his adopted daughter, getting to know each other and growing as people in the face of adversity. It''s a shining example of writing for a video game, where even the blatant illusion of choice doesn''t dampen the immersion due to the exciting scenarios, profound questions and lovely characterisation. But with each successive game, the grounded and human core eroded, replaced by flashy and overly dramatic set pieces capitalising on shock value to keep the excitement high. Character artists, animators, level designers, sound engineers, composers, so many wildly talented people thrown at a cynical project to capitalise on a recognisable name. It''s an endless shame that Telltale Games met the end it did; all its talented workers simply dumped on the streets one unremarkable day while the corporation was kept on life support to keep promises. Not promises made to employees hired less than a week earlier, but to shareholders.'),
