	 ('195283672','76561197965398617','2427430','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'Not sure what''s everyones''s problem with the game. It''s a FREE update to owners of this game. A game that is 15 years old. Or a cheap price for first time buyers. It''s a 15 years old game and this is an ENHANCED version of it. Not a remake. OF COURSE it''s a new coat of paint, it''s a coat of paint on 15 years old game. It actually runs better than the old game on newer OS/systems. I am very happy to play again without having to install 50 mods to make it look like this and manage the stability issues that goes with modding old games. I paid NOTHING and i am very HAPPY !'),
	 ('9577725','76561197980283507','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'After really enjoying Poker Night at the Inventory (Poker Night 1) I thought well do I really need another poker game. Surely that one would do right. Well eventually at a slightly reduced price I jumped at Poker Night 2 and I regret never getting it sooner and at full price. 

This game is far better. 

The humour is better. 

The poker engine itself seems better. 

Poker Night 2 introduces Omaha poker which I have only really played a couple of times as I find it easier to work with a two card hand than a five but if you have a favourite type of poker you can stick with that and maybe occassionally switch to the other just to mix it up a bit.

Even though theres only still a fairly limited but certainly less limited amount of lines the other characters say it is varied enough to keep those same lines interesting, and the characters refer more to you the player which brings you into the immersion more. You can buy players drinks to uncover their bluffs or make them have poor judgements. Although subjective I do prefer the characters playing here than on the first game. Brock Samson was not to familiar with before playing but it did not stop me enjoying his character enough for me to look into the Venture Brothers.

It certainly is not going to be a game that you are going to play until hell freezes over but if you want the thrills of poker without the cash loss with a mix of the feeling you are playing a video game not random chance simulator 1995. I would certainly say $5 is well spent here.'),
