	 ('90277867','76561198807995154','73190','20251230',0,0,0,0,10,'As with the rest of this series from AGEOD, the learning curve for this game is rather steep, particularly as this is a detailed economic as well as military simulation, but it is well worth the time investment. The simulation of the time period is well-done and evocative, the AI is a credible opponent, and I particularly like the lovely board game-like unit cards and artwork. The UI definitely takes some getting used to, but I already understood the military, logistical, and events systems from playing To End All Wars, so for me it was really just a matter of learning the economic and political aspects. This is a deep and complex game and isn''t for everyone, but if you''re looking for a deep simulation of the 1850-1920 period, this is a very solid title, and one which I believe deserves an eventual sequel on AGEOD''s new engine.'),
	 ('151424188','76561197975859740','339740','20251230',1,0,0,0,8,'If you don''t like big Melons and wanna play with cute Pussycats. This game is for you! ;-)'),
	 ('81532581','76561197966924959','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,3,'Can''t set how much they talk, game also pauses even in window mode, so you can''t tab to another window while the "players" keeps yapping on. Claptrap is incredible annoying. The conversation halts the game sometimes and you gotta finish for their rambling to end. A vastly lesser version than the first game.'),
	 ('39198093','76561198263622438','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,5,'Crashes in the middle of games - won''t deal.'),
	 ('32299834','76561197960912290','500670','20251230',2,0,0,0,9,'For the right price this should be worth it to people who are looking for a ridiculous bullet hell like shooter. Lucky thing is, this game is so simple that you don''t need 2 hours to experience the entire game SO you can easily buy, try for an hour and if you DON''T like it, return it. The store page mentions ''upgrades'' but I have no idea what they''re talking about. You play 30 - 60 second rounds before dying from some un-seen attack, the rounds are complete chaos. The execution is excellent, but the depth is void. What you see in the trailers is exactly what you get, ''upgrades'' are more like achievements from what I can see, but the games way too hard to get any of them for me. It does have local co-op, runs just fine. The game seems to have no video options, it automatically selects the resolution it wants. All in all it is a fun game, my only complaint is after playing Kingdoms, a game that appears to be very simple but in actuality is hiding tremendous depth, this is actually as shallow as it appears. My only hope for this game in it''s long run will be to add workshop support for the ''arenas''. As it seems to stand now there are 2, maybe 3 arenas. There are no ''levels'', where you start is where you die, just one big rectangle of a single doorless room. You run in circles blasting until you die. When you restart the background can change. Even in the hour I played, I got rather bored with the two backgrounds in the game, they are extremely simple. There feels like a serious lack of investment in game assests here. For the $6.80 I paid for this on sale, I feel like I barely got what I paid for, fun gameplay aside, there really is very little game content, I feel like the bulk of what I paid was to cover the cost of the intro video, because that''s where the majority of the development effort feels like it went.'),
	 ('1870917','76561197999109761','234710','20251230',0,0,0,0,0,'This game was my first-ever "Not Recommended" review back when the ability to write a negative review first became a feature on Steam. I decided to give it another go a few years later to see if I''d feel differently about it now.

Sadly, my mind hasn''t changed. It''s just inferior to the original in every way.

- The characters included aren''t nearly as good as those in the first game, and their appearances are ripped straight from their games of origin, making them all clash horribly with each other. (Two of them aren''t even game characters - Brock Samson is from a TV show, and Ash Williams, at the time of release, hadn''t been seen in *any* digital media in years. Why is he even here?)
- The banter isn''t nearly as funny, and the same conversations tend to repeat multiple times in a single game.
- Any and all dialog directed at / mentioning the player is used to insult them, which ranges from gentle ribbing to "I''m starting to think the writers for this game held genuine contempt for their customer base." (They also made all of GLaDOS'' dialog incredibly derogatory, which ignores her entire character arc from Portal 2. And the fact that she didn''t hate *everyone* - she only hated Chell because she destroyed her.)
- Animations are super buggy, with characters frequently jumping from one pose to another with no transition, repeating the same motion multiple times in a row, or just generally moving in incredibly unnatural-looking ways.
- The unlocks for other games aren''t nearly as good (plus the items you win from the characters in this game aren''t even the things you unlock!)
- The ability to skip dialog from the first game has been removed, and the characters often won''t take their turn until a conversation ends, meaning you can be stuck on one character''s turn for *a full minute* with no way to bypass it.
- Navigating menus is about as easy as pulling your own teeth. There are a whole bunch of separate menu screens with multiple-seconds-long transitions (really just a black screen), and  you have to move through them in the prescribed order. The main menu is, ironically, the hardest to reach of them all.

Just a total downgrade. Play the first one instead.'),
