	 ('175777995','76561198096896853','3110820','20251230',1,0,0,0,10,'A Retro-Inspired Adventure with Modern Touches

One of the game''s strongest points is its smooth controls. Navigating through the levels feels intuitive and responsive, which is crucial for a platformer that demands precision. Jumping, shooting, and executing special moves are all seamlessly integrated.

The level designs are thoughtfully crafted, offering a mix of platforming challenges and enemy encounters and I found this kept the gameplay engaging. The bosses are particularly noteworthy. Each one presents unique attack patterns and requires a strategic approach to defeat. I found these fights challenging but fair.

The soundtrack of Crisis Command is a standout feature. The music complements the action perfectly and enhance the retro atmosphere. 

The game''s pixel art is clean and simple, evoking a classic aesthetic that fans of the genre will appreciate.  But there are moments where I thought the art style could benefit from additional detail, particularly in the character portraits. More intricate visuals could have added depth and personality to the characters, making them more memorable.

While Crisis Command shines in many aspects, it does have a few shortcomings. I found the in-game text could be hard to read, which definitely impacted my immersion in the game. Additionally, a few areas of the levels felt a bit empty, featuring larger open spaces without enemies or environmental challenges.  I found this disrupted the pacing and made the levels feel less dynamic.

Overall, I think the game successfully captures the essence of classic shooter/platformers while introducing modern enhancements and offers a fun and engaging experience that is well worth your time.
'),
