	 ('34371733','76561198029765447','234710','20251230',2,0,0,0,10,'The AI in this game is definitely cheating.

Now, before you lecture me on probability and statistics, I didn''t mean anything about it. I said the game is cheating not because that I lost a 90+% chance showdown, but because the AI dare to all-in on such poor cards. And guess what, everytime they did this, they won. It is not about randomness, not about why they get the last card dealt in their favor, but about the AI''s decision making. If you have played a few games, you will certainly see that the AI''s decision are based on the final outcome, but not on currently dealt cards.

[h1]Proof[/h1]
[url=https://telltale.com/community/discussion/45903/poker-night-2-cheats-outrageously][quote=maestropendejo]I have numbers to backup 65 games that I have played. That is after I played anywhere from 75-125 games on my XBOX. I started tracking it because I was noticing an alarming trend, in a showdown scenario the NPC I was playing against was getting the EXACT card they needed like it was magical bullshit.[/quote][/url]

[h1]My theory (TL;DR they cheat for sure)[/h1]
The staff at Telltale Game said: "The game has no motivation to cheat." But on the contrary, I think they have the very reason to cheat: they want to make this game "harder" without actually spending a lot of time coding the AI. It''s a lot easier to give AI the perfect information and then pretend that they lost a few, than to actually deal with probability and bluffing.

My experience with other poker-vs-AI games tells me, if you bluff every single time, you will almost certainly win most games.
Why? Because first, if they have bad or average cards, they don''t have the confidence to bet a large sum. Second, if they all fold, they don''t get to see your cards, so they don''t get the information required to "understand" your trick. And third, even if they get information (like when you lost if they have better cards and went to a showdown), as probability stands, every round is independent. Just because you bluffed last round doesn''t mean your all-in decision this round have no cards to back it.
You may wonder why human players won''t lose to such simple trick? Because we have "memory", we keep track of everyone''s playstyle. If you are bluffing every single time/most of the time, then we will assume that you are bluffing if you made a big bet again. But actually develop such an AI which has "memory", and which can analyse your playstyle and adjust their tactics based on your playstyle, is very difficult and requires a lot of computational resources (which involves machine learning and that kind of stuff). So in most games the AI will use the same tactic without considering your playstyle. This is not only true to poker, but to many different games. And this is why most AI are "weak" in a sense, and why most "difficult" AI cheat one way or another. (Btw, if they make an AI capable of learning, your game will break if you let your friend play it XD)

[h1]Conclusion[/h1]
All in all, it is the AI that''s the real problem, not some probability bullsh*t (did Telltale pay you guys or something?) It ruins the fun of this game.'),
