	 ('196116992','76561197981638563','2427430','20251230',3,0,0,0,10,'[h1]“[i]As I get older, I just prefer to knit.[/i]”
~Tracey Ullman[/h1]

Call of Pripyat was announced in 2009. On the same day, we received our first official confirmation of a sequel. In his interview, Sergiy Grygorovych discussed the numerous ideas he and his team had and how few of them actually made it into their products. It''s important for us to know this because it helps us make sense of why all three original S.T.A.L.K.E.R. games are so heavily different from each other. The first one felt like an evolution of ideas seen in Venom: Codename Outbreak. It was GSC''s very first attempt to make a "true" open-world game (while having pretty big maps, Venom was still a mission-based thing), and naturally, they made some obvious mistakes, such as a lackluster story (aside from the endings) and a rather empty world. The second game, Clear Sky, aimed to fix the empty world problem. Now, the third one, Call of Pripyat? It was supposed to push into story.

In other words, the whole thing was very experimental. GSC didn''t have a rock-solid vision for their series and just kept throwing things in. And you know what? It was a good thing. Why? Because it made S.T.A.L.K.E.R. games feel more alive. Usually, when something becomes popular, we start getting more of exactly the same, right? The Call of Duty series was proving that point back in the day. S.T.A.L.K.E.R. was nothing like that, and every new game felt a lot like a whole new experience. So... Let''s take a look at what GSC gave us this time.

As I already said, the main goal here was to polish the single-player experience, to turn the game into something deeper, something more narrative. Unlike Clear Sky, Call of Pripyat takes place after the first game''s events, and yes, we''ll meet our old pal Strelok again. Don''t expect him to be the main character, though. Our guy this time is an undercover military officer whose goal is to investigate why the military operation to take control of the Chernobyl Nuclear Power Plant failed. By casting us as an outsider and weaving a more intricate main quest, GSC aimed to transform Call of Pripyat into a deeper single-player experience, at least on paper. On paper at least. And it''s more than just words. See, unlike previous games, this one features Fallout-like composite endings. In other words, doing the optional objectives will actually have an impact on what you''ll see in the end. This helps quite a lot since S.T.A.L.K.E.R. was never known for its bright characters, and personally, I always had a hard time caring about them. They all looked somewhat similar to me. Extra motivation is exactly what I needed. Moreover, GSC added some sort of a reputation system, while letting us pass time by sleeping. We won''t be able to sleep anywhere, unfortunately, and the only way to do so is to use a bed in certain locations, but hey! It''s a thing. A good one too, since we''ve got quite a lot of “wait for a bit and return later” objectives.

In other words, we have a lot of good things on paper. Unfortunately, as a developer, GSC didn''t grow that much. Even the in-engine cutscenes that were finally added (both previous games only had text, which made them pretty anticlimactic) feel rather rough and cheap, while of course the game was incredibly buggy. And it was a problem. A big one. Those who were ready to overlook technical difficulties already had two games to mod for their liking and kept investing time into them. While those who just wanted a smooth and polished experience were skeptical to begin with. I mean, it''s S.T.A.L.K.A.R.. Of course, it''s going to be rough and half-broken. And well, that''s exactly what Call of Pripyat was. Rough and half-broken. It still had everything that made S.T.A.L.K.E.R. games special and more, but it was clear that enough was enough. It was time for a true sequel. Still, quite a lot of people named Call of Pripyat the best S.T.A.L.K.E.R. game out there, and I can certainly see why. But like I said, at the moment it came out, the world had already moved on. Just... to return to this game again years later.

And again, I was quite excited about the remaster. Just because I always wanted to give this game another chance. This time without all the bugs. And you know what? Among all three original S.T.A.L.K.E.R. games, this one felt the most smooth in its Enhanced Edition form. I didn''t encounter a single crash and I didn''t experience a single major bug during the main questline. Which is especially good thanks to how I was playing on my TV with a gamepad (like all other Enhanced versions, this one comes with native gamepad support). Did I enjoy my time, though? Well...

Let''s not talk about our usual problems with the Enhanced Editions for too long. Despite some people refusing to face reality, the largest war since WWII is ongoing, and naturally, such things come with some consequences. This includes the removal of soviet symbolism, similar to how swastikas were handled in German versions of games not so long ago. And it''s not like this was a great version to begin with. It''s yet another quick overhaul that, despite its technological advancements, may actually look worse than the originals (think Mafia 2 and Life is Strange). It needs to be said, but what about the actual game? The actual experience? Well, it did not age well. Even on its initial launch, it felt rather rough around the edges, while now? Let''s just say that among the three games, Call of Pripyat may look the most annoying one out there, simply due to its mechanics. Like, it''s the only game among the three that requires you to search for things without providing an objective marker during the main quest. It''s hard not to notice that GSC kept learning, and level design, for example, started to turn from Venom-like open spaces into something more real, but somehow, most of the things that were good on paper ended up contributing to that certain annoying aftertaste.

And that''s the irony here. While back in the day there was no real need for Call of Pripyat to exist because fans already had two games to play with, nowadays there was no real need for the Enhanced Edition. Just because a modded version of the original can technically provide you with better results, as long as you don''t mind losing native gamepad support and rumble. Still, I''m kind of thankful for this. Not only because GSC gave the Enhanced Editions away for free to everybody who owned the originals (again, originals were removed from the store, but if you buy the Enhanced versions, you''ll get those as a free bonus), but also because I''m one of those people who rarely get into mods. "And the older I get, the more I prefer a “casual” approach. I prefer my games to work out of the box. I click the install button, pick up the gamepad, and expect the game to work. If you''re like that too? This here is the version for you. Also, again, on paper it is better than at least Shadow of Chernobyl. Still, with this game aging poorly, this ends up being a very niche product. Casual players will surely have more fun playing the technically inferior Shadow of Chernobyl, while hardcore stalkers, like I already said, will stick with the original anyway. Notably, the original even boasts co-op/multiplayer through mods, while in Enhanced Edition multiplayer was completely removed. Still, when it comes to free content, one can''t be too picky. GSC did achieve what I wanted them to do and gave me the chance to experience this game on my TV with no major bugs to ruin my experience. It''s certainly something. Does this version really deserve a thumbs up? Not really, no. But it doesn''t deserve a thumbs down either. It''s just... It didn''t aim for the PC audience, okay? It was a way to finally give console players a chance to experience the famous saga. While PCs? PCs just got it because why not. And it''s better than not having it at all, so let''s be thankful.'),
