	 ('11014766','76561198002404741','234710','20251230',1,0,0,0,10,'It sucks that Steam uses a Recommend/Not Recommend system, becaues this is actually about to be an in-depth look at the game, and readers are probably going to assume I just think anyone should play it because I decided to recommend it.

But, since no one likes the guy who presses the thumbs down, I''m going to try as hard as I can to let you, the reader, know what you''re getting. Because [i]Poker Night 2 isn''t for everyone[/i]. But, you may very well be that person, and this reviewer says, [b]you most likely are[/b].

I''d go really overly-in-depth, because it''s my preferred critical style, but Steam has a text limit I''m wasting...with a superfluous sentence, anyways, [b]here''s what you really [i]need[/i] to know about Poker Night 2[/b]. 
(Spoiler alert: It''s really not that much, just a few things.)

1: It is absolutely sequel to Poker Night at the Inventory, and it has changed a lot of things.
Now, the game presents the really cool new changes in an interesting and cool way, so I''ll put this part in a spoiler. Don''t worry, it''s not some kind of big story-driven game, I just respect the way that they knew people would come from the first Poker Night expecting the same thing, and they have a fun way of introducing the new stuff. As a gamer, I implore you to skip this if you care about Poker Night as an experience, but encourage you to read it if you''re trying to be careful about your money.
[spoiler] 

Change A: $20,000 buy-ins starting with 400 - 800 blinds.
The game no longer limits you to a puny $10,000 with starting blinds of 200 - 400. All the virtual preliminaries have been multiplied by x2. Little thing, but I think it was a great idea. It makes everything feel that much more valuable and, literally, high stakes.

Change B: Inventory tokens.
Your proficiency is rewarded with tokens. The tokens can be used to buy new chips, decks, and felts, as well as to buy drinks for each character, making them infinitely usable. Buying drinks for other players increases the amount and clarity of their tells, or for non-experienced poker players or the layman, it makes the characters tipsy and makes them unable to keep a focused poker face. A pokused facer foke. A hocus pocus trace.
Sorry.
And, like I said, they are used as currency towards getting new chips, decks, and felts. Like the first game, each of them are based off of the 5 new characters, and in order of how much their sets cost: Borderlands, Sam & Max, Venture Bros., Army of Darkness, and Portal. That''s 15 things to buy. And, the felts now change the look of the inventory, as well as character appearances, and tons of new dialogue for having different felts and combining the chips, decks, and felts of a single canon.
The amount of tokens you get are as follows:
5th place - 0
4th place - 10
3rd place - 20
2nd place - 50
1st place - 100
The cheapest item is the Borderlands chipset for 20 tokens, and the most expensive is the Portal felt for 480. Buying drinks also costs 5 tokens per character.
So, that pretty much covers tokens.

Change C: No dialogue options.
Other than subtitles and a mute option, you''re stuck watching/listening to the animations and dialogue of each character, even if that slows down/gets in the way of gameplay. I''ll talk more about that later.

Change D: Bounty Challenges
Again, I''ll talk about this one later, but I''m sure many of you desire Poker Night''s TF2 and/or Borderlands items, and they''re not the same as the first time around. Read more, because things have changed, and you''ll want to hear about it, trust me.

[/spoiler]

So, moving on.

2: Don''t buy it if you [i]know[/i] you don''t like poker games.
Unless you''re one of those people playing for the items, in which case I direct you to skip this and go directly to #3. Otherwise, if you''re thinking about buying this game for any other reason, make sure you''re anywhere above a hater of poker if you want it. If you''ve never dealt a hand in your life, or you like/are experienced in the dealing, I give this game a seal of absolute approval. The AI is designed not to give you a realistic feeling of the psychology of poker, but rather the video game alternative. Experienced poker players and ignorants like me before I played the first Poker Night will have fun learning how each character tells, plays, and values their gut over their mind. It is a [i]great[/i] game for learning how to play poker, and on its own right as a fun game to figure out how to be good at. Looking at card games as a gamer is like looking at the cavemen figure out how to match star patterns as a scientist: humble beginnings, and fun. You can play Texas Hold ''Em or its alternate, Omaha Hold ''Em. 
[i][b]*Important note for noobs, poker itself is not a card game, but an array of card games played similarly with changing rules depending on the name of the game. Texas and Omaha Hold ''Em are the two variants you play in Poker Night 2.[/b][/i]
Both games are extremely similar and friendly to newcomers, and the game includes a tutorial on the game accessible at any time, as well as a cheat sheet which shows the differnt kinds of hands and the hierarchy of their value. 
And for the pros, you''ll find that the experience of having AI in the place of human combatants isn''t entirely jarring, and fun in its own right thanks to their good design and fun personalities.

So, if you absolutely know for a fact that you cannot stand Poker by all means testable, stay far, far, far away from Poker Night 2. It is practically nothing BUT virtual high-stakes poker.
But, for everyone else, hop right in. That is, except for...

3: The poor, poor item junkies that buy this game to get the items.
You guys are in for a real crapshoot. Unlike the 1st game, you can''t just start a new game over and over until you get the chance to knock one guy out for the item. It''s MUCH harder this time around. You are assigned 3 random "Bounty Challenges", that you [i]must[/i] complete before you can get a chance at getting the item. But, if you want a specific item, lucky for you, because you can actually just start a new tournament over and over until the character puts up the item you want, and you can try over and over forever. Sound nice right?

Well, unfortunately, it can''t be that simple.

First of all, the Portal item can only be acquired after collecting [i]all[/i] the bounty items. Trust me, it''s a headache. Because the challenges couldn''t be simple things like "Win a tournament" or "Buy drinks for everyone."

There are those options, and thank god there are. You can expect to get 3 levels of difficulty from the random challenges: "Easy gimmes, Hard, Extremely Improbable".

You might end up with the "Come in Fourth place" or "Buy everyone drinks" challenge, but expect it to come with: "Steal a hand on the turn" and "Win with only a high card."

It may not sound too hard now, but trust me: The 24 hours of gameplay I currently have at the time of writing this review has been done SOLELY to get those items. I am currently on the Portal bounty item. I spent all of my free time just yesterday, as well as 3 hours this morning, ONLY, I repeat, [b]ONLY[/b] on a SINGLE challenge: Win a hand with just a high card.

For you item-seekers, trust me: this is going to be a very. Very. [i]Very. Very. [b]Very. Bueller.[/b][/i] ...hard experience.

If I could get back all the time I wasted getting those items, I would. [b]It doesn''t feel worth it at all.[/b]

If you''ve got a lot of time with nothing productive or of superceding responsilbility to do, and you''ve got enough patience to justify literally [b]days[/b] of being insulted by computers, then I leave it up to you to assume the green light. Otherwise, I advise you to do the right thing and go outside instead.

Anyways, I''ve reached the text limit, so here''s my final statement:
I definitely recommend Poker Night 2 for everyone except item-seekers and poker-haters.'),
